/* AI brain Q&A pack 28: sports & games */
(window.AIQA = window.AIQA || []).push(
  ["explain offside | offside rule simple | why was that offside", "Offside stops attackers from just camping by the goal. A player is offside if, at the moment a teammate plays the ball to them, they're in the opponent's half and nearer the goal line than both the ball and the second-to-last defender (usually the last outfield player). Being level counts as onside, and you can't be offside from a throw-in, goal kick or corner."],
  ["is it offside from a throw in | can you be offside from a corner | offside from goal kick", "No! You **can't be offside** directly from a throw-in, a corner kick or a goal kick. Offside only counts when the ball is played by a teammate in normal open play or from a free kick."],
  ["what is a penalty kick in soccer | when do you get a penalty in soccer | penalty kick rules", "A **penalty kick** is given when a defender commits a direct free-kick foul (like tripping, pushing or a handball) inside their own penalty area. The ball goes on the penalty spot 11 meters (12 yards) from goal, and it's one kicker against the goalkeeper, who must stay on the goal line until the ball is kicked."],
  ["what is a penalty shootout | how does a penalty shootout work", "A **penalty shootout** breaks a tie in a knockout match after extra time. Each team takes five penalty kicks, alternating. If they're still level after five each, it goes to sudden death, one kick each, until one team scores and the other misses."],
  ["what is a free kick in soccer | direct vs indirect free kick | difference between direct and indirect free kick", "A **free kick** restarts play after a foul. From a **direct** free kick you can score straight into the goal. From an **indirect** free kick, the ball must touch another player before a goal counts; the referee shows this by holding an arm straight up."],
  ["what is a yellow card | what is a red card | yellow and red cards in soccer", "A **yellow card** is a caution for things like reckless fouls, time-wasting or arguing with the referee. Two yellows in one match equal a **red card**, which means the player is sent off and the team plays with one fewer player. A straight red is shown for serious fouls or violent behavior."],
  ["what are the positions in soccer | soccer positions explained | football positions", "The main soccer positions are:\n- **Goalkeeper**: guards the goal and can use hands in the penalty area\n- **Defenders**: center-backs and full-backs who stop attacks\n- **Midfielders**: link defense and attack, pass and win the ball\n- **Forwards / strikers**: score goals\nWingers play wide on the flanks to cross or cut inside."],
  ["what does a striker do | what is a striker in soccer | what is a center forward", "A **striker** (or center forward) is the main goal scorer, playing furthest up the field. They try to find space, hold up the ball for teammates and finish chances with their feet or head."],
  ["what does a goalkeeper do | soccer goalie rules | can the goalie use hands anywhere", "The goalkeeper's job is to stop shots and organize the defense. They may handle the ball only inside their own **penalty area**. They can't pick up a ball deliberately kicked back to them by a teammate, and they shouldn't hold it for more than about six seconds."],
  ["what is a defender in soccer | what does a center back do | what is a full back", "**Center-backs** play in the middle of the defense, marking strikers, heading away crosses and blocking shots. **Full-backs** play on the left and right sides, defending wingers and often running forward to support attacks. A **wing-back** is a full-back with an even more attacking role."],
  ["what is a midfielder | what does a midfielder do | what is a defensive midfielder", "Midfielders are the engine of the team. A **defensive midfielder** shields the defense and wins the ball back, a **central midfielder** passes and controls tempo, and an **attacking midfielder** (the number 10) creates chances behind the strikers."],
  ["what is a winger in soccer | what does a winger do", "A **winger** plays wide on the left or right of the attack. They use speed and dribbling to beat defenders, then cross the ball into the box or cut inside to shoot."],
  ["how long is a soccer match | how long is a soccer game | how many halves in soccer", "A professional soccer match is **90 minutes**: two halves of 45 minutes with a 15-minute half-time break. The referee adds **stoppage time** at the end of each half for injuries, substitutions and delays. Youth games are usually shorter."],
  ["what is extra time in soccer | what is stoppage time | what is injury time", "**Stoppage time** (or injury time) is the few minutes added at the end of each half to make up for time lost to injuries, substitutions and other delays. **Extra time** is different: two extra 15-minute halves played in knockout games when the score is tied after 90 minutes."],
  ["what is a handball in soccer | handball rule soccer | when is it a handball", "A **handball** is when a player (other than the goalkeeper in their own box) deliberately touches the ball with their hand or arm, or makes their body unnaturally bigger with their arm. The arm starts at the bottom of the armpit, so shoulders are allowed."],
  ["what is a corner kick | when do you get a corner in soccer", "A **corner kick** is given to the attacking team when the defending team last touches the ball before it crosses their own goal line (without a goal being scored). The ball is placed in the corner arc and kicked into play, and you can score directly from it."],
  ["what is a goal kick | when is it a goal kick", "A **goal kick** is given to the defending team when the attacking team last touches the ball before it goes over the goal line without scoring. The ball is kicked from anywhere inside the goal area."],
  ["what is a throw in | how do you do a throw in | throw in rules", "When the ball goes out over a sideline, the other team gets a **throw-in**. You must face the field, keep part of both feet on or behind the line, use both hands and throw the ball from behind and over your head."],
  ["what is var in soccer | what does var stand for | video assistant referee", "**VAR** stands for **video assistant referee**. Officials watching replays can advise the referee on clear and obvious mistakes in four situations: goals, penalties, straight red cards and mistaken identity."],
  ["what is a clean sheet | what does clean sheet mean", "A **clean sheet** means a team (and its goalkeeper) didn't let in any goals during a match. In the US it's often called a **shutout**."],
  ["what is an own goal | what does own goal mean", "An **own goal** is when a player accidentally puts the ball into their own team's net. The goal counts for the other team."],
  ["what is a nutmeg in soccer | what does nutmeg mean", "A **nutmeg** is when a player passes or dribbles the ball through an opponent's legs and collects it on the other side. It's one of the cheekiest skills in soccer!"],
  ["what is a bicycle kick | what is an overhead kick", "A **bicycle kick** (or overhead kick) is when a player throws their body into the air backward and kicks the ball over their own head while upside down. It's spectacular but tricky, so practice on soft ground!"],
  ["what is a formation in soccer | what does 4-4-2 mean | what is 4-3-3", "A **formation** describes how a team lines up, counted from defense to attack (the goalkeeper isn't counted). **4-4-2** means four defenders, four midfielders and two strikers. **4-3-3** uses three midfielders and three forwards for a more attacking style."],
  ["what is a header in soccer | how do you head a soccer ball", "A **header** is when a player hits the ball with their forehead. Keep your eyes open, tense your neck, and strike the ball with the flat part of your forehead. Young players usually practice with very light balls or not at all, because repeated heading isn't good for growing brains."],
  ["what is a dribble in soccer | how to dribble a soccer ball", "**Dribbling** is running with the ball while keeping it close using small touches. Use both feet, the inside and outside of your foot, keep your head up to see the field, and change speed and direction to beat defenders."],
  ["what is a through ball | what is a cross in soccer", "A **through ball** is a pass played into the space behind the defenders for a teammate to run onto. A **cross** is a pass from the side of the field into the penalty area, aiming for a teammate to head or volley it in."],
  ["what is a volley in soccer | what is a half volley", "A **volley** is when you kick the ball while it's still in the air, before it touches the ground. A **half volley** is when you kick it just as it bounces up off the ground."],
  ["what is a soccer field called | how big is a soccer field | what is the penalty area in soccer", "A soccer field is called a **pitch**. It's roughly 100 to 110 meters long and 64 to 75 meters wide for adult matches. The **penalty area** (or box) is the big rectangle in front of each goal, and the smaller rectangle inside it is the **goal area** (or six-yard box)."],
  ["how many substitutes in soccer | how many subs can you make in soccer", "In many professional competitions teams can make **five substitutions** in a match, using up to three stoppages in play (plus one more chance at half-time). Youth and amateur leagues often allow unlimited rolling substitutions."],
  ["what is futsal | futsal rules", "**Futsal** is a fast indoor version of soccer played on a hard court with five players per team (including the goalkeeper) and a smaller, heavier ball that doesn't bounce much. It's great for improving close control and quick passing."],
  ["what is a foul in basketball | basketball fouls explained | personal foul", "A **foul** is illegal physical contact, like pushing, holding, hacking an arm or blocking someone's path. A **personal foul** is charged to the player, and if a player gets fouled while shooting, they get **free throws**. Too many fouls and a player must leave the game (fouling out)."],
  ["how many fouls to foul out | how many fouls before you foul out in basketball", "In the NBA a player fouls out after **6 personal fouls**. In college, high school and international (FIBA) basketball it's **5 fouls**."],
  ["what is a technical foul | what is a tech in basketball", "A **technical foul** is for unsportsmanlike behavior that isn't about physical contact during play, like arguing with the referee, taunting or delaying the game. The other team usually gets a free throw."],
  ["what is a flagrant foul | what is an unsportsmanlike foul", "A **flagrant foul** (called an **unsportsmanlike foul** in international play) is unnecessary or excessive contact, such as a hard swing at someone's head. The fouled team gets free throws and keeps the ball, and serious ones get a player ejected."],
  ["what is charging in basketball | charge vs block | what is a blocking foul", "If a defender has set their feet in a legal position and the dribbler crashes into them, it's **charging** (an offensive foul). If the defender is still moving into the dribbler's path or arrives too late, it's a **blocking** foul on the defender."],
  ["what is a free throw | how many points is a free throw | free throw line", "A **free throw** is an unguarded shot from the free-throw line, 15 feet (about 4.6 m) from the backboard, awarded after certain fouls. Each made free throw is worth **1 point**."],
  ["what is a three pointer | how far is the three point line | 3 pointer basketball", "A **three-pointer** is a basket made from behind the three-point arc, worth **3 points**. Baskets inside the arc are worth 2. The arc is about 23 feet 9 inches (7.24 m) from the hoop in the NBA and 6.75 m in FIBA, a bit closer in the corners."],
  ["what is an and one | what does and 1 mean in basketball", "An **and-one** is when a player is fouled while shooting but the shot still goes in. The basket counts, and they get **one extra free throw**, giving the chance for a three-point play (or four-point play on a three-pointer)."],
  ["what are the positions in basketball | basketball positions explained | 1 through 5 positions", "The five basketball positions are:\n1. **Point guard**: runs the offense and handles the ball\n2. **Shooting guard**: scores from outside\n3. **Small forward**: versatile scorer and defender\n4. **Power forward**: strong inside player and rebounder\n5. **Center**: usually the tallest, protects the rim and rebounds"],
  ["what does a point guard do | what is a point guard", "The **point guard** is like the team's quarterback on the court. They bring the ball up, call plays, pass to open teammates and set the pace of the game."],
  ["what does a center do in basketball | what is a center in basketball", "The **center** usually plays closest to the basket. They grab rebounds, block shots, score near the rim and set screens for teammates."],
  ["what is traveling in basketball | what is a travel | what is double dribble", "**Traveling** is taking too many steps while holding the ball without dribbling, or moving your pivot foot. A **double dribble** is dribbling with both hands at once, or stopping your dribble and then starting again. Both give the ball to the other team."],
  ["what is the shot clock | how long is the shot clock", "The **shot clock** limits how long a team can hold the ball before shooting. In the NBA and FIBA it's **24 seconds**, and the shot must hit the rim before time runs out. College basketball uses a 30-second shot clock."],
  ["what is a rebound | offensive vs defensive rebound", "A **rebound** is grabbing the ball after a missed shot. A **defensive rebound** is when the defending team gets it, ending the attack. An **offensive rebound** is when the shooting team gets it back for another chance."],
  ["what is a layup | what is a dunk | layup vs dunk", "A **layup** is a close-range shot where you jump and gently lay the ball in, often off the backboard. A **dunk** is when a player jumps high enough to push the ball down through the hoop."],
  ["what is a pick and roll | what is a screen in basketball", "A **screen** (or pick) is when a player stands still to block a defender's path so a teammate can get free. In a **pick and roll**, a player sets a screen for the ball handler, then rolls toward the basket to receive a pass."],
  ["what is a triple double | what is a double double", "A **double-double** is reaching 10 or more in two stat categories (like points and rebounds) in one game. A **triple-double** is 10 or more in three categories, often points, rebounds and assists."],
  ["what is an assist | what is a steal | what is a turnover in basketball", "An **assist** is a pass that leads directly to a teammate scoring. A **steal** is taking the ball away from the other team. A **turnover** is losing possession through a bad pass, traveling or similar mistake."],
  ["what is goaltending | what is basket interference", "**Goaltending** is blocking a shot that's on its way down toward the basket or touching it after it hits the backboard above the rim. The basket counts anyway. **Basket interference** is touching the ball or rim while the ball is on or in the basket."],
  ["what is the three second rule | three seconds in the key | what is the paint", "The **paint** (or key) is the painted rectangle under the basket. An offensive player can't stay in it for more than **3 seconds** at a time. In the NBA, defenders also can't camp there for more than 3 seconds unless guarding someone closely."],
  ["what is a zone defense | man to man defense | zone vs man to man", "In **man-to-man** defense, each defender guards a specific opponent. In a **zone** defense, each defender guards an area of the court, like a 2-3 zone with two players up top and three near the basket."],
  ["how long is a basketball game | how many quarters in basketball | how long is an nba game", "NBA games have **four 12-minute quarters**. FIBA international games have four 10-minute quarters, and US college men's games have two 20-minute halves. If tied, they play 5-minute overtime periods."],
  ["how high is a basketball hoop | how tall is a basketball rim", "A regulation basketball rim is **10 feet (3.05 m)** above the floor. Many youth leagues use lower hoops, around 8 or 9 feet, for younger players."],
  ["what is a jump ball | how does a basketball game start", "A basketball game starts with a **jump ball** (tip-off): the referee tosses the ball up between two players at center court and each tries to tap it to a teammate."],
  ["what is a backcourt violation | over and back in basketball | 8 second rule", "Once a team gets the ball past half court, it can't take it back into its own half; that's a **backcourt violation**. Teams also have **8 seconds** to move the ball past half court in the NBA and FIBA (10 seconds in some other levels)."],
  ["what is 3x3 basketball | 3 on 3 basketball rules", "**3x3 basketball** is played on a half court with three players per team and one hoop. Baskets inside the arc are worth 1 point and outside the arc 2 points, with a 12-second shot clock. In official play the first team to 21 points (or the leader after 10 minutes) wins."],
  ["how do you shoot a basketball | basketball shooting form | how to shoot better in basketball", "Try the **BEEF** method: **B**alance (feet shoulder-width apart), **E**yes on the rim, **E**lbow tucked under the ball, **F**ollow through by snapping your wrist like reaching into a cookie jar. Practice close to the hoop first, then move back."],
  ["what was pong | tell me about pong | who made pong", "**Pong** was a simple table-tennis video game released by **Atari** in **1972**. Two players moved paddles up and down to bounce a square \"ball\" back and forth. It was one of the first commercially successful video games and helped launch the whole video game industry."],
  ["what was the arcade era | golden age of arcade games | what is an arcade", "The **golden age of arcade games** was roughly the late 1970s to the mid-1980s. People played coin-operated machines in arcades, chasing high scores on classics like **Space Invaders** (1978), **Pac-Man** (1980), **Donkey Kong** (1981) and **Galaga** (1981)."],
  ["tell me about pac man | how do you play pac man | who are the pac man ghosts", "In **Pac-Man** (1980), you guide a yellow circle through a maze eating dots while avoiding four ghosts: **Blinky**, **Pinky**, **Inky** and **Clyde**. Eating a **power pellet** turns the ghosts blue so you can eat them for bonus points. It's one of the most famous video games ever."],
  ["what was space invaders | tell me about space invaders", "**Space Invaders** (1978, by Taito) had players moving a laser cannon left and right to shoot rows of descending aliens before they reached the ground. The aliens sped up as you destroyed more of them. It was a massive hit that helped start the arcade boom."],
  ["what was the first home video game console | magnavox odyssey | first game console", "The first home video game console was the **Magnavox Odyssey**, released in **1972**. It used plastic overlays on the TV screen and had no sound. Later the **Atari 2600** (1977) made swappable game cartridges popular."],
  ["what was the video game crash of 1983 | video game crash", "In **1983**, the North American video game market crashed. There were too many consoles and too many low-quality games, so people stopped buying. The industry recovered a few years later, largely thanks to the **Nintendo Entertainment System** (NES), which focused on quality control."],
  ["history of nintendo | how did nintendo start | was nintendo a card company", "**Nintendo** was founded in **1889** in Kyoto, Japan, making **hanafuda** playing cards. Over the years it tried toys and other businesses before becoming a video game giant with arcade games and home consoles like the **NES** (Famicom in Japan) in the 1980s."],
  ["who is mario | history of super mario | when was mario created", "**Mario** first appeared in the arcade game **Donkey Kong** (1981), where he was called Jumpman. He was created by game designer **Shigeru Miyamoto**. **Super Mario Bros.** (1985) on the NES became one of the best-selling and most influential games of all time."],
  ["what was the game boy | handheld game history | first handheld game console", "Early handheld games included Mattel's electronic sports games and Nintendo's **Game & Watch** (1980). The **Game Boy** (1989) became hugely popular with its swappable cartridges and **Tetris**. Later handhelds added color screens, touchscreens and 3D."],
  ["what is tetris | who invented tetris | how do you play tetris", "**Tetris** was created in **1984** by Soviet software engineer **Alexey Pajitnov**. Falling shapes called **tetrominoes** (made of four squares) drop into a well, and you rotate and move them to complete horizontal lines, which then disappear. If the stack reaches the top, the game ends."],
  ["what are console generations | video game console generations explained", "Consoles are often grouped into **generations**:\n- **1st**: early 1970s, simple built-in games like Pong\n- **2nd**: late 1970s cartridge systems like the Atari 2600\n- **3rd**: 8-bit era, like the NES\n- **4th**: 16-bit era, like the Super Nintendo and Sega Genesis\n- **5th**: early 3D and CDs, like the PlayStation and Nintendo 64\n- Later generations added online play, HD graphics, motion controls and digital downloads"],
  ["what does 8 bit mean | what is 16 bit graphics | what is pixel art", "**8-bit** and **16-bit** describe the processors in older consoles like the NES (8-bit) and Super Nintendo (16-bit). The terms now also describe their blocky, colorful **pixel art** style, which many modern indie games still use on purpose."],
  ["what was the console war | sega vs nintendo", "In the late 1980s and 1990s, **Sega** and **Nintendo** competed fiercely in the so-called **console war**. Sega's **Genesis** (Mega Drive) and its speedy mascot **Sonic the Hedgehog** went head to head with the **Super Nintendo** and Mario."],
  ["when did 3d video games start | first 3d games | what was the nintendo 64", "Fully 3D games became mainstream in the mid-1990s with consoles like the **Sony PlayStation**, **Sega Saturn** and **Nintendo 64**. Games like **Super Mario 64** (1996) showed how characters could move freely in 3D worlds, with an analog stick for smooth control."],
  ["what is a game cartridge | cartridge vs disc", "A **cartridge** is a plastic case with a chip that stores a game, which you plug into a console. Cartridges load fast and are tough. **Discs** (CDs, DVDs and Blu-rays) hold much more data and were cheaper to make. Today many games are also downloaded digitally."],
  ["what is a fighting game | what is a racing game | what is a rhythm game", "**Fighting games** have two characters battling one-on-one using special moves and combos. **Racing games** are about speed on tracks, from realistic simulators to kart racers with power-ups. **Rhythm games** have you press buttons, dance or play instruments in time with music."],
  ["what is a puzzle game | what is a simulation game | what is a sports video game", "**Puzzle games** test your brain with logic, matching or physics challenges. **Simulation games** imitate real activities like farming, flying planes or running a city. **Sports games** let you play soccer, basketball and more on screen."],
  ["what is a metroidvania | what is a stealth game | what is a survival game", "A **metroidvania** is a 2D exploration game with a big interconnected map, where new abilities unlock new areas. In **stealth games**, you sneak past enemies without being seen. In **survival games**, you gather resources, craft tools and stay alive."],
  ["what is an mmo | what does mmorpg mean | what is a battle royale game", "An **MMO** (massively multiplayer online) game lets huge numbers of players share one online world; an **MMORPG** is an MMO role-playing game. A **battle royale** drops many players into a shrinking map, and the last player or team standing wins."],
  ["what is a visual novel | what is an idle game | what is a tower defense game", "A **visual novel** is a story-driven game told through text and pictures, where your choices change the story. An **idle game** keeps making progress even when you're not playing. In **tower defense**, you place towers along a path to stop waves of enemies."],
  ["what is a first person game | first person vs third person games | what does fps stand for in games", "In a **first-person** game, you see through your character's eyes. In a **third-person** game, the camera is behind or above your character so you can see them. **FPS** can mean **first-person shooter** or **frames per second**, how smoothly a game runs."],
  ["what is a boss in a video game | what is a boss fight | what is a mini boss", "A **boss** is a powerful enemy, often at the end of a level or world, that's tougher than normal enemies and usually has attack patterns to learn. A **mini-boss** is a smaller challenge partway through a level. Beating bosses often unlocks new abilities or areas."],
  ["what is a power up | what is a 1 up | what is an extra life", "A **power-up** is an item that gives your character a temporary or permanent boost, like extra speed, strength or invincibility. A **1-up** (or extra life) gives you another chance after you lose, a tradition from arcade games."],
  ["what is a checkpoint in a game | what is a save point | what does respawn mean", "A **checkpoint** is a spot in a level where you restart if you fail, so you don't go back to the very beginning. A **save point** lets you save your progress. **Respawn** means your character reappears after being defeated."],
  ["what is an easter egg in a video game | what is a hidden level", "An **Easter egg** is a secret hidden in a game by its creators, like a hidden message, a funny reference or a secret room. One of the first famous ones was in the Atari game **Adventure** (1980), where the designer hid his name in a secret room."],
  ["what is the konami code | what is a cheat code", "A **cheat code** is a secret input that unlocks something special, like extra lives. The most famous is the **Konami Code**: **Up, Up, Down, Down, Left, Right, Left, Right, B, A** (sometimes followed by Start). It first appeared in the 1980s in Konami games."],
  ["what is lag in games | what does ping mean in games", "**Lag** is a delay between your action and what happens on screen, often caused by a slow internet connection in online games. **Ping** measures how long data takes to travel to the game server and back, in milliseconds; lower is better."],
  ["what does pvp mean | what does pve mean | what is co op in games", "**PvP** means **player versus player**, competing against other people. **PvE** means **player versus environment**, fighting computer-controlled enemies. **Co-op** (cooperative) means players team up to work together."],
  ["what is a glitch in a video game | what is a bug in a game", "A **glitch** or **bug** is a mistake in a game's code that makes something happen that wasn't intended, like falling through the floor or a character getting stuck. Some glitches are so fun that players use them on purpose, especially speedrunners."],
  ["what is a d pad | what is a joystick | parts of a game controller", "A **D-pad** (directional pad) is the cross-shaped button for moving in four directions, made famous by Nintendo. A **joystick** or **analog stick** lets you move smoothly in any direction. Controllers also have face buttons, shoulder buttons (bumpers) and triggers."],
  ["what is a level in a video game | what is a side quest | what is a main quest", "A **level** (or stage) is one section of a game, often with a goal to reach. The **main quest** is the central storyline. **Side quests** are optional extra missions that give rewards, lore or fun detours."],
  ["what is a sprite | what is a hitbox", "A **sprite** is a 2D image used for a character or object in a game, like Mario in older games. A **hitbox** is an invisible shape around a character or object that the game uses to check whether things touch, like whether an attack hits."],
  ["what is an indie game | what is a video game engine", "An **indie game** is made by a small independent team or even one person, without a big publisher. A **game engine** is software that provides tools like graphics, physics and sound so creators can build games more easily."],
  ["how are video games made | jobs in video games | what does a game designer do", "Making a game takes many roles: **game designers** plan the rules and levels, **programmers** write the code, **artists** create characters and worlds, **sound designers** and composers make audio, **writers** create the story and **testers** find bugs. Small games can be made by one person, while big ones need hundreds."],
  ["how do i make my own video game | how to start making games", "Start small! Try beginner-friendly tools like **Scratch** (drag-and-drop coding), then move on to game engines as you learn. Make a tiny game first, like a maze or a simple platformer, finish it and then make the next one a bit bigger."],
  ["what is a high score | why do arcade games have high scores | what does initials on high score mean", "A **high score** is the best score achieved on a game. Arcade machines saved the top scores, and players entered three **initials** to claim their spot, which made people keep coming back to beat each other."],
  ["what is a text adventure | what was zork", "A **text adventure** (or interactive fiction) is a game played entirely with words: it describes where you are, and you type commands like \"go north\" or \"take lamp.\" **Zork** (late 1970s) is one of the most famous examples."],
  ["what is a motion control game | what is vr gaming | what is augmented reality gaming", "**Motion controls** let you play by moving your body or a controller, like swinging a remote as a tennis racket. **Virtual reality** (VR) uses a headset to put you inside a 3D world. **Augmented reality** (AR) adds game objects on top of the real world, seen through a phone or glasses."],
  ["what is a retro game | what are retro games", "A **retro game** is an older game, often from the arcade, 8-bit or 16-bit eras, that people still enjoy. Many classic games have been re-released on newer systems, and collectors keep old consoles and cartridges working."],
  ["what is a pinball machine | how does pinball work", "**Pinball** is an arcade game where you use flippers to keep a metal ball in play on a slanted table full of bumpers, ramps and targets that score points. It existed long before video games, and pinball machines are still popular today."],
  ["what was tennis for two | what was spacewar", "**Tennis for Two** (1958) was an early electronic game made by physicist William Higinbotham, showing a side view of a tennis court on an oscilloscope screen. **Spacewar!** (1962) was created at MIT, with two spaceships battling around a star, and it inspired early arcade games."],
  ["how do i get better at puzzle games | how to get better at tetris", "For puzzle games, slow down and look for patterns. In **Tetris**, keep your stack flat, leave one column open for the long I piece to clear four lines at once (a **Tetris**), and use the next-piece preview to plan ahead."],
  ["what is a level up | what is a skill tree", "**Leveling up** means your character reaches a new level after earning enough experience, usually becoming stronger. A **skill tree** is a branching chart of abilities you can unlock, letting you customize how your character plays."],
  ["what is a loot box | what is in game currency | what are microtransactions", "**In-game currency** is pretend money used inside a game. **Microtransactions** are small real-money purchases inside a game. A **loot box** is a random prize box; since you don't know what you'll get, it's wise to always ask a parent before spending real money in any game."],
  ["what are video game ratings | what does rated e mean | game age ratings", "Video games have **age ratings** to help families choose. In North America, the ESRB uses ratings like **E** (Everyone), **E10+** (10 and up), **T** (Teen) and **M** (Mature 17+). Europe uses **PEGI** with age numbers like 3, 7, 12, 16 and 18. Check the rating and talk with a parent."],
  ["how many innings in baseball | what is an inning | how long is a baseball game", "A standard baseball game has **9 innings**. Each inning has a **top** half (the visiting team bats) and a **bottom** half (the home team bats), and each half ends after three outs. If it's tied after nine, they play extra innings. There's no game clock."],
  ["what counts as a strike | how many strikes is an out", "A **strike** is a pitch the batter swings at and misses, a pitch in the strike zone they don't swing at, or a foul ball (when they have fewer than two strikes). **Three strikes** and the batter is out, which is called a **strikeout**."],
  ["what is a ball in baseball | what is a walk | how many balls is a walk", "A **ball** is a pitch outside the strike zone that the batter doesn't swing at. **Four balls** give the batter a **walk** (base on balls), and they go to first base for free."],
  ["what is the strike zone | where is the strike zone", "The **strike zone** is the area over home plate from about the middle of the batter's torso down to just below the knees. A pitch that passes through any part of it counts as a strike if the batter doesn't swing."],
  ["what are the positions in baseball | baseball positions numbers | how are baseball positions numbered", "The nine fielding positions, with their scorekeeping numbers:\n1. Pitcher\n2. Catcher\n3. First base\n4. Second base\n5. Third base\n6. Shortstop\n7. Left field\n8. Center field\n9. Right field"],
  ["what does a shortstop do | what is a shortstop", "The **shortstop** plays between second and third base. Lots of ground balls go their way, so they need quick feet, a strong arm and good reflexes. They also help cover second base on double plays."],
  ["what does a catcher do in baseball | what is the catcher", "The **catcher** crouches behind home plate, catches pitches, signals to the pitcher what to throw, guards home plate and tries to throw out runners stealing bases. They wear a mask, chest protector and shin guards."],
  ["what does a pitcher do | types of pitches in baseball | what is a curveball", "The **pitcher** throws the ball to the batter from the mound, trying to get strikes and outs. Common pitches include the **fastball** (speed), **curveball** (big downward bend), **slider** (sharp sideways break) and **changeup** (looks like a fastball but arrives slower)."],
  ["what is a foul ball | is a foul ball a strike", "A **foul ball** is a batted ball that lands outside the foul lines. It counts as a strike if the batter has 0 or 1 strikes, but a foul can't be the third strike (except a foul bunt). If a fielder catches a foul ball in the air, the batter is out."],
  ["what is a single double triple | what is a base hit", "A **base hit** is when the batter hits the ball and safely reaches base without an error. A **single** gets them to first base, a **double** to second, a **triple** to third, and a **home run** all the way around."],
  ["what is a grand slam | what is a grand slam in baseball", "A **grand slam** is a home run hit when the bases are loaded (runners on first, second and third), scoring **4 runs** at once, the most possible on one swing."],
  ["what is an rbi | what does rbi mean", "**RBI** stands for **runs batted in**. A batter earns an RBI for each run that scores because of their hit, walk, sacrifice or similar play (but not on an error or a double play)."],
  ["what is a bunt | what is a sacrifice bunt", "A **bunt** is when the batter holds the bat out and lightly taps the ball into the infield instead of swinging. A **sacrifice bunt** gives up the batter's chance to get on base so a runner can move up a base."],
  ["what is a stolen base | how do you steal a base", "A **stolen base** is when a runner advances to the next base while the pitcher is delivering the pitch, without the ball being hit. They have to beat the catcher's throw to the base."],
  ["what is a double play | what is a triple play", "A **double play** is a single play where the defense gets **two outs**, like fielding a ground ball, tagging second base and throwing to first. A **triple play** gets three outs at once and is very rare."],
  ["what is a tag out | what is a force out | how do you get outs in baseball", "Ways to get outs include a strikeout, catching a fly ball, a **force out** (touching a base before a runner who is forced to run there) and a **tag out** (touching a runner with the ball when they're off a base)."],
  ["what is the infield fly rule | infield fly rule explained", "The **infield fly rule** says that with runners on first and second (or bases loaded) and fewer than two outs, an easy pop-up in the infield makes the batter automatically out. It stops fielders from dropping the ball on purpose to get a cheap double play."],
  ["what is an error in baseball | what does e mean in baseball", "An **error** is charged to a fielder when they make a mistake, like dropping an easy catch or making a bad throw, that lets a batter or runner reach base or advance when they should have been out."],
  ["what is a no hitter | what is a perfect game | no hitter vs perfect game", "A **no-hitter** is a complete game where the pitchers allow zero hits. A **perfect game** is even rarer: no batter reaches base at all, meaning no hits, walks, hit batters or errors, 27 up and 27 down."],
  ["what is a batting average | how do you calculate batting average", "**Batting average** is hits divided by official at-bats. If you get 3 hits in 10 at-bats, your average is .300. A .300 hitter is considered very good, because even great hitters fail most of the time!"],
  ["what is era in baseball | earned run average", "**ERA** (earned run average) shows how many earned runs a pitcher allows per nine innings. Lower is better. It's calculated as earned runs times 9, divided by innings pitched."],
  ["what is the bullpen | what is a relief pitcher | what is a closer", "The **bullpen** is where relief pitchers warm up. A **relief pitcher** comes in after the starting pitcher. The **closer** is the reliever who usually pitches the final inning to protect a lead, earning a **save**."],
  ["what is the seventh inning stretch | why do people stand in the 7th inning", "The **seventh-inning stretch** is a baseball tradition where fans stand up and stretch between the top and bottom of the seventh inning, often singing \"Take Me Out to the Ball Game.\""],
  ["what is softball | difference between softball and baseball", "**Softball** is like baseball but with a larger ball, a smaller field and pitches thrown **underhand**. Fastpitch games are usually 7 innings. The bases are 60 feet apart instead of 90."],
  ["what is t ball | what is tee ball", "**T-ball** is a beginner version of baseball for young kids. Instead of a pitcher, the ball sits on a tee and the batter hits it from there, so everyone gets to make contact and learn the basics."],
  ["what is a down in football | how do downs work | first down explained", "In American football, the offense gets **four downs** (plays) to move the ball **10 yards**. If they make it, they get a fresh **first down** and four more tries. If not, the other team gets the ball. Teams usually punt on 4th down instead of risking it."],
  ["what does 3rd and 5 mean | what does 1st and 10 mean | down and distance", "The first number is the **down** (which try it is) and the second is the **distance** in yards needed for a new first down. **3rd and 5** means it's the third try and they need 5 more yards. **1st and 10** is the start of a new set of downs."],
  ["how many points is a touchdown | what is a touchdown", "A **touchdown** is worth **6 points**. You score one by carrying the ball into the opponent's end zone or catching a pass while inside it. Then the team tries an extra point (1 point, kicked) or a two-point conversion."],
  ["what is a field goal | how many points is a field goal", "A **field goal** is worth **3 points**. The kicker kicks the ball from the ground (held by a teammate) through the upright goalposts above the crossbar. Teams try one when they're close enough but can't get a first down."],
  ["what is a two point conversion | extra point vs two point conversion", "After a touchdown, a team can kick an **extra point** for 1 point, or try a **two-point conversion** by running or passing the ball into the end zone again from close range. It's riskier but worth 2."],
  ["what is a safety in football | how many points is a safety", "A **safety** is worth **2 points** to the defense. It happens when the offense is tackled (or commits certain fouls) in its own end zone. The team that gave up the safety then has to kick the ball to the other team."],
  ["how is american football scored | football scoring explained", "American football scoring:\n- **Touchdown**: 6 points\n- **Extra point** (kick): 1 point\n- **Two-point conversion**: 2 points\n- **Field goal**: 3 points\n- **Safety**: 2 points"],
  ["what are the positions in american football | football positions explained | nfl positions", "Teams have three units:\n- **Offense**: quarterback, running back, wide receivers, tight end and offensive linemen\n- **Defense**: defensive linemen, linebackers, cornerbacks and safeties\n- **Special teams**: kicker, punter, returners and long snapper"],
  ["what does a quarterback do | what is a qb | quarterback role", "The **quarterback** (QB) leads the offense. They receive the snap, call plays, hand off to running backs or throw passes to receivers. Good quarterbacks read the defense quickly and make smart decisions under pressure."],
  ["what does a running back do | what is a wide receiver | what is a tight end", "A **running back** takes handoffs and runs with the ball. **Wide receivers** run routes and catch passes downfield. A **tight end** lines up next to the offensive line and both blocks and catches passes."],
  ["what does the offensive line do | what is a lineman", "The **offensive line** (center, two guards and two tackles) protects the quarterback from defenders and blocks to open running lanes. The **center** also snaps the ball to start each play."],
  ["what is a linebacker | what is a cornerback | what does a safety position do", "**Linebackers** play behind the defensive line and tackle runners or cover short passes. **Cornerbacks** cover wide receivers. **Safeties** are the last line of defense deep in the field, stopping long passes and runs."],
  ["what is a sack in football | what is an interception | what is a fumble", "A **sack** is tackling the quarterback behind the line of scrimmage before they throw. An **interception** is when a defender catches a pass meant for the offense. A **fumble** is when a ball carrier drops the ball, and either team can recover it."],
  ["what is a punt | why do teams punt on 4th down", "A **punt** is a kick where the punter drops the ball and kicks it before it hits the ground, sending it far downfield. Teams usually punt on 4th down so that if they can't make a first down, the other team starts much farther from scoring."],
  ["what is a kickoff | what is a touchback", "A **kickoff** starts each half and follows scores: one team kicks the ball to the other. A **touchback** happens when the ball goes into or through the end zone and isn't returned, so the receiving team simply starts from a set yard line."],
  ["what is the line of scrimmage | what is a snap in football", "The **line of scrimmage** is the imaginary line across the field where the ball sits before a play; neither team can cross it until the play begins. The **snap** is when the center passes the ball back between their legs to start the play."],
  ["what is a flag in football | common football penalties | what is holding", "When a referee throws a yellow **flag**, it means a penalty. Common ones: **holding** (grabbing an opponent illegally), **false start** (an offensive player moving before the snap), **offside** (a defender crossing the line early) and **pass interference** (illegally blocking a receiver from catching the ball). Penalties move the ball back or forward by yards."],
  ["how long is a football game | how many quarters in football", "An NFL game has **four 15-minute quarters**, but the clock stops often, so games usually take over three hours. There's a halftime break after the second quarter, and overtime if tied."],
  ["how big is a football field | how long is a football field", "An American football field is **100 yards** long between the goal lines, plus a **10-yard end zone** at each end, so 120 yards total. It's 53⅓ yards wide, with yard lines marked every 5 yards."],
  ["what is flag football | flag football rules", "**Flag football** is a no-tackle version of American football. Each player wears flags on a belt, and instead of tackling, defenders pull a flag off the ball carrier to end the play. It's safer and great for beginners."],
  ["what is a hail mary in football | what is a hail mary pass", "A **Hail Mary** is a very long, desperate pass thrown toward the end zone, usually at the end of a half or game, hoping a teammate can catch it among a crowd of defenders."],
  ["what is the red zone in football | what is the end zone", "The **end zone** is the 10-yard scoring area at each end of the field. The **red zone** is the area between the opponent's 20-yard line and their goal line, where the offense has a great chance to score."],
  ["what is the super bowl | super bowl explained | what is the super bowl concept", "The **Super Bowl** is the championship game of the NFL, American professional football. The winners of the two conferences (AFC and NFC) play one game to decide the champion. It's one of the most-watched events in the US, famous for its halftime show and commercials."],
  ["what is the difference between american football and rugby | football vs rugby", "In **American football**, play stops after each tackle, forward passes are allowed, and players wear helmets and pads. In **rugby**, play flows continuously, passes must go backward, and players wear little padding."],
  ["what is canadian football | canadian vs american football", "**Canadian football** is very similar to American football but uses a larger field (110 yards between goal lines), 12 players per side instead of 11, and only **three downs** instead of four to gain 10 yards."],
  ["what is icing in hockey | icing rule | what does icing mean", "**Icing** is when a team shoots the puck from its own half all the way across the opponent's goal line without anyone touching it. Play stops and the faceoff goes back to the offending team's end. A team that is short-handed (on the penalty kill) is allowed to ice the puck."],
  ["what is a power play in hockey | what is a penalty kill | what is shorthanded", "When a player gets a penalty, they sit in the penalty box and their team plays one player short. The team with the extra player is on the **power play**, and the short-handed team is on the **penalty kill**. A goal scored by the short-handed team is a **shorthanded goal**."],
  ["what is offside in hockey | hockey offside rule", "In hockey, an attacking player is **offside** if they cross the opponent's **blue line** into the attacking zone before the puck does. Play stops and there's a faceoff outside the zone."],
  ["how long is a hockey game | how many periods in hockey", "A professional ice hockey game has **three 20-minute periods**, with intermissions between them. If tied, there may be overtime and sometimes a **shootout**."],
  ["what are the positions in hockey | hockey positions explained", "An ice hockey team has six players on the ice: a **goaltender**, two **defensemen** and three **forwards** (a **center** and a left and right **wing**). Players switch on and off in quick shifts because skating is so tiring."],
  ["what is a faceoff in hockey | how does a hockey game start", "A **faceoff** starts play: the referee drops the puck between two opposing players (usually centers), who try to win it back to a teammate. Faceoffs also restart play after stoppages."],
  ["what is the penalty box | how long are hockey penalties | minor vs major penalty", "The **penalty box** is where a player sits after a foul. A **minor penalty** (like tripping or hooking) lasts **2 minutes** and ends early if the other team scores. A **major penalty** (like fighting) lasts **5 minutes** and doesn't end early."],
  ["what is a hockey puck made of | how big is a hockey puck", "A hockey puck is made of hard **vulcanized rubber**, 3 inches (7.6 cm) wide and 1 inch thick. Pucks are often frozen before games so they bounce less."],
  ["what is pulling the goalie | why do hockey teams pull the goalie", "When a team is losing late in a game, it may **pull the goalie**: take the goaltender off the ice and add an extra skater. It improves the chance of scoring but leaves an empty net."],
  ["what is a slap shot | what is a wrist shot | types of hockey shots", "A **slap shot** is a hard, powerful shot where the player raises the stick and slams it into the puck. A **wrist shot** uses a quick flick of the wrists for accuracy. A **snapshot** is a mix of both, and a **backhand** uses the back of the blade."],
  ["what is a hockey shootout | how does a shootout work in hockey", "A **shootout** breaks a tie after overtime in some leagues. Players take turns skating in alone against the goalie. Usually each team gets three shooters, then it goes to sudden death."],
  ["what is a hockey rink | how big is a hockey rink | lines on a hockey rink", "A hockey rink is a sheet of ice surrounded by boards, about 200 by 85 feet in the NHL. It has a red **center line**, two **blue lines** dividing the zones, and red **goal lines** near each end, plus faceoff circles."],
  ["what is checking in hockey | what is body checking", "**Body checking** is using your hip or shoulder to knock an opponent off the puck. It's legal in many adult leagues but banned in youth hockey and women's hockey, and hits to the head or from behind are penalties."],
  ["what is a zamboni | why do they clean the ice", "A **Zamboni** (or ice resurfacer) is a machine that drives over the rink, shaves off the rough top layer, scoops up the snow and lays down a thin layer of warm water that freezes smooth."],
  ["what is ringette | what is broomball", "**Ringette** is an ice sport where players use straight sticks to pass and shoot a rubber ring, and players can't carry the ring over the blue lines. **Broomball** is played on ice in shoes instead of skates, using broom-like sticks and a ball."],
  ["what does love mean in tennis | why is zero called love in tennis", "In tennis, **love** means **zero**. Nobody knows for sure where it came from; one popular theory says it comes from the French \"l'oeuf\" (the egg), because a zero looks like an egg."],
  ["why is tennis scored 15 30 40 | tennis points explained | how do tennis points work", "Tennis points go **love (0), 15, 30, 40**, then game. One theory is that scores were once kept on a clock face moving 15, 30, 45, and 45 was shortened to 40. You need to win a game by **two points**."],
  ["what is deuce in tennis | what is advantage in tennis", "**Deuce** is when both players reach 40-40. From deuce, a player must win two points in a row: the first gives them **advantage**, and the next wins the game. If they lose the next point, it's back to deuce."],
  ["how does a tiebreak work in tennis | what is a tiebreaker in tennis", "When a set reaches **6-6**, players usually play a **tiebreak**. Points are counted 1, 2, 3 and so on, and the first to **7 points** with a lead of two wins the set (7-6). Serves alternate every two points."],
  ["how many sets in tennis | how do you win a tennis match | what is a set in tennis", "A **set** is won by the first player to win **6 games** with a two-game lead (or by a tiebreak at 6-6). Most matches are **best of three sets**, while men's matches at Grand Slams are best of five."],
  ["what is an ace in tennis | what is a double fault", "An **ace** is a serve so good the opponent can't even touch it, winning the point right away. A **fault** is a serve that misses the service box; you get a second chance, but two faults in a row is a **double fault**, and you lose the point."],
  ["what is a let in tennis | let serve", "A **let** is called when a serve clips the top of the net and still lands in the correct service box. The serve is simply replayed without penalty."],
  ["what are tennis strokes | forehand backhand volley | types of tennis shots", "Main tennis shots:\n- **Forehand**: hit on your dominant side\n- **Backhand**: hit across your body, one- or two-handed\n- **Serve**: starts each point\n- **Volley**: hit before the ball bounces, usually near the net\n- **Lob**: a high shot over the opponent\n- **Drop shot**: a soft shot that lands just over the net\n- **Smash**: an overhead slam"],
  ["what is a grand slam in tennis | what are the four tennis majors", "The four **Grand Slam** tournaments (majors) are the **Australian Open** (hard court), **French Open** (clay), **Wimbledon** (grass) and **US Open** (hard court). Winning all four in one calendar year is called a calendar Grand Slam."],
  ["what are the different tennis court surfaces | clay vs grass vs hard court", "**Clay** courts are slow with high bounces, favoring long rallies. **Grass** courts are fast with low, skidding bounces, helping big servers. **Hard courts** are in between and the most common."],
  ["what is a break of serve | what is break point in tennis", "Serving is an advantage, so winning a game while your opponent serves is called a **break** of serve. A **break point** is when the receiver is one point away from breaking."],
  ["what is pickleball | how do you play pickleball | pickleball rules", "**Pickleball** mixes tennis, badminton and table tennis. It's played on a small court with solid paddles and a plastic ball with holes. Serves are underhand, the ball must bounce once on each side before volleying, and you can't volley inside the no-volley zone (the **kitchen**). Games usually go to 11, win by 2."],
  ["what is padel | how is padel different from tennis", "**Padel** is a racket sport played in doubles on an enclosed court smaller than a tennis court, with glass walls you can play the ball off, like squash. It uses solid stringless paddles and underarm serves, and the scoring is the same as tennis."],
  ["what is squash | how do you play squash", "**Squash** is played by two players in a four-walled indoor court. Players take turns hitting a small rubber ball against the front wall, which must hit above the tin before bouncing once on the floor. Games are usually played to 11 points."],
  ["what is a birdie in golf | what is an eagle in golf | golf scores names", "Golf scores compared to **par**:\n- **Albatross** (double eagle): 3 under par\n- **Eagle**: 2 under par\n- **Birdie**: 1 under par\n- **Par**: the expected score\n- **Bogey**: 1 over par\n- **Double bogey**: 2 over par"],
  ["what is par in golf | what does par mean | par 3 par 4 par 5", "**Par** is the number of strokes a skilled golfer is expected to need for a hole or a whole course. Holes are usually **par 3, 4 or 5**, depending on their length. A typical 18-hole course has a par of about 72."],
  ["what is a hole in one | what is an ace in golf", "A **hole in one** (or ace) is when a golfer hits the ball from the tee straight into the hole with one shot. It usually happens on short par-3 holes and is a rare and exciting moment."],
  ["what are golf clubs called | types of golf clubs | driver iron putter wedge", "Golfers carry up to **14 clubs**:\n- **Driver**: hits the ball furthest off the tee\n- **Woods and hybrids**: long shots from the fairway\n- **Irons** (numbered 3 to 9): medium shots, higher number means higher and shorter\n- **Wedges**: short, high shots and getting out of sand\n- **Putter**: rolling the ball on the green"],
  ["what is a fairway | what is the rough | what is a green in golf", "The **tee box** is where you start each hole. The **fairway** is the short-cut grass leading to the green. The **rough** is longer grass beside the fairway. The **green** is the very short grass around the hole where you putt. Bunkers are sand traps, and hazards include water."],
  ["what is a handicap in golf | how do golf handicaps work", "A golf **handicap** is a number that shows a player's skill level, roughly how many strokes over par they usually shoot. It lets players of different abilities compete fairly: the higher-handicap player gets extra strokes."],
  ["what is match play | what is stroke play | match play vs stroke play", "In **stroke play**, you count every shot over the whole round, and the lowest total wins. In **match play**, players compete hole by hole, and whoever wins the most holes wins the match."],
  ["why do golfers yell fore | what does fore mean in golf", "Golfers shout **\"Fore!\"** to warn people that a ball might be heading toward them. If you hear it, cover your head and duck!"],
  ["what is a mulligan in golf | what does mulligan mean", "A **mulligan** is an informal do-over: retaking a bad shot without counting it. It's not allowed in official rules, but friends sometimes use it in casual rounds."],
  ["what is mini golf | how do you play mini golf | putt putt rules", "In **mini golf** (putt-putt), you only use a putter on short, fun holes with obstacles like windmills, ramps and tunnels. Count your strokes on each hole, and the lowest total wins. Many courses cap each hole at about 6 strokes."],
  ["what is disc golf | how do you play disc golf", "**Disc golf** is like golf but you throw a flying disc into a metal basket with hanging chains. You count your throws on each hole and try for the lowest total, just like regular golf."],
  ["what are the positions in volleyball | volleyball positions explained", "Volleyball positions:\n- **Setter**: sets up the ball for attackers, like a quarterback\n- **Outside hitter**: main attacker on the left side\n- **Opposite hitter**: attacks from the right side\n- **Middle blocker**: blocks at the net and hits quick sets\n- **Libero**: defensive specialist in a different-colored jersey who digs and passes"],
  ["what is a libero in volleyball | why does the libero wear a different jersey", "The **libero** is a back-row defensive specialist who's great at passing and digging. They wear a different color so referees can spot them, because they can't attack the ball above the net height, can't serve in some leagues and can swap in and out without counting as substitutions."],
  ["how is volleyball scored | what is rally scoring | how many points to win volleyball", "Volleyball uses **rally scoring**: a point is scored on every rally, no matter who served. Sets go to **25 points** (win by 2), and matches are best of five sets, with the fifth set played to 15."],
  ["what is a bump set spike | volleyball skills | what is a dig in volleyball", "The three classic volleyball touches are **bump** (pass with forearms), **set** (push the ball up with fingertips for a hitter) and **spike** (jump and hit the ball down hard). A **dig** is saving a hard-hit ball near the floor, and a **block** is jumping at the net to stop a spike."],
  ["what is rotation in volleyball | why do volleyball players rotate", "In volleyball, whenever your team wins the serve back from the other team, players **rotate** one position clockwise. This way everyone takes turns serving and playing both front and back row."],
  ["what is beach volleyball | beach volleyball rules", "**Beach volleyball** is played on sand, usually with **two players** per team. Sets go to 21 points, matches are best of three (third set to 15), and players can't use open-handed tips to push the ball over."],
  ["what is a shuttlecock | what is a birdie in badminton", "A **shuttlecock** (also called a shuttle or **birdie**) is the object hit in badminton. It has a rounded cork base and a skirt of feathers or plastic, which makes it slow down quickly and always fly cork-first."],
  ["how is badminton scored | how many points in badminton", "Badminton uses rally scoring: you score a point on every rally. A game goes to **21 points**, with a 2-point lead needed (capped at 30). Matches are best of three games."],
  ["what is a smash in badminton | badminton shots | what is a clear in badminton", "Key badminton shots: the **smash** (a fast, steep downward hit), the **clear** (a high, deep shot to the back of the court), the **drop shot** (a soft shot that falls just over the net) and the **drive** (a fast, flat shot)."],
  ["how is table tennis scored | ping pong scoring | how many points in ping pong", "Table tennis games go to **11 points**, and you need to win by 2. Players switch serve every **2 points** (every point at 10-10). Matches are usually best of five or seven games."],
  ["table tennis serve rules | how do you serve in ping pong", "In table tennis, toss the ball up at least 16 cm (about 6 inches) from an open, flat palm and hit it as it falls. In singles it must bounce once on your side, then once on your opponent's side. If it clips the net and lands correctly, it's a let and is replayed."],
  ["what is spin in table tennis | topspin vs backspin", "**Topspin** makes the ball dip down and jump forward after bouncing. **Backspin** (or underspin) makes it float and stay low, and it tends to go into the net if you don't adjust. Sidespin curves the ball sideways."],
  ["what is a wicket in cricket | what does wicket mean", "**Wicket** has several meanings in cricket: the set of three wooden **stumps** with two **bails** on top, the pitch itself, and the dismissal of a batter (\"taking a wicket\"). A team's innings ends when 10 of its 11 batters are out."],
  ["how do you get out in cricket | ways to get out in cricket", "Common ways a batter gets out:\n- **Bowled**: the ball hits the stumps\n- **Caught**: a fielder catches the hit ball before it bounces\n- **LBW**: the ball would have hit the stumps but hit the batter's leg\n- **Run out**: the stumps are broken before the batter reaches the crease\n- **Stumped**: the wicketkeeper breaks the stumps while the batter is out of the crease"],
  ["what is lbw in cricket | leg before wicket", "**LBW** means **leg before wicket**. A batter is out if the ball would have hit the stumps but was blocked by their leg or body instead of the bat. There are extra conditions about where the ball pitched and hit them."],
  ["what is a four and a six in cricket | how do you score runs in cricket", "Batters score **runs** by running between the wickets after hitting the ball. If the ball reaches the boundary after bouncing, it's a **four**. If it clears the boundary without bouncing, it's a **six**."],
  ["what is an over in cricket | how many balls in an over", "An **over** is a set of **six legal deliveries** bowled by one bowler from one end. After each over, a different bowler bowls from the other end."],
  ["what is test cricket | what is t20 | cricket formats", "Cricket has three main formats:\n- **Test cricket**: up to five days, two innings each\n- **One Day Internationals (ODIs)**: 50 overs per side\n- **T20**: 20 overs per side, about three hours, fast and high-scoring"],
  ["what is a century in cricket | what is a duck in cricket", "A **century** is when a batter scores **100 runs** in one innings, and a half-century is 50. A **duck** is getting out for zero runs; out first ball is a **golden duck**."],
  ["what is a no ball | what is a wide in cricket", "A **no ball** is an illegal delivery, often because the bowler's front foot overstepped the crease. A **wide** is a ball too far from the batter to hit. Both give the batting team an extra run, and the ball has to be bowled again."],
  ["what are cricket fielding positions | what is slip in cricket", "Cricket has many fielding positions with fun names, like **slip** and **gully** (close behind the batter to catch edges), **point**, **cover**, **mid-on**, **mid-off**, **square leg** and **fine leg**. The **wicketkeeper** stands behind the stumps wearing gloves."],
  ["what is a spin bowler | spin vs fast bowling", "**Fast bowlers** rely on speed, bounce and swing. **Spin bowlers** bowl slower but twist the ball with their fingers or wrist so it turns sharply after bouncing, tricking the batter."],
  ["what is a try in rugby | how is rugby scored | rugby scoring", "Rugby union scoring:\n- **Try** (grounding the ball over the goal line): 5 points\n- **Conversion** kick after a try: 2 points\n- **Penalty kick** through the posts: 3 points\n- **Drop goal**: 3 points\nIn rugby league, a try is worth 4 points and a drop goal 1."],
  ["what is a scrum in rugby | how does a scrum work", "A **scrum** restarts play after minor infringements like a knock-on. The forwards from each team bind together and push against each other, and the ball is fed into the tunnel between them. Players hook it backward with their feet to win possession."],
  ["what is a lineout in rugby | what is a ruck | what is a maul", "A **lineout** restarts play when the ball goes out of touch: players line up and teammates lift a jumper to catch the throw. A **ruck** forms when the ball is on the ground and players push over it. A **maul** is when the ball carrier is held up while players bind around them."],
  ["what is a knock on in rugby | why can't you pass forward in rugby", "A **knock-on** is when a player drops or knocks the ball forward; it gives a scrum to the other team. In rugby you can only pass **backward or sideways**, so teams gain ground by running and kicking."],
  ["rugby union vs rugby league | difference between rugby union and league", "**Rugby union** has 15 players per side, with rucks, mauls and contested scrums. **Rugby league** has 13 players and uses a tackle count: after six tackles, the ball goes to the other team, similar to downs in American football."],
  ["what is rugby sevens | sevens rugby", "**Rugby sevens** is a fast version of rugby union with **7 players** per side on a full-size field. Halves are just 7 minutes long, so it's full of speed and long runs. It's an Olympic sport."],
  ["what are rugby positions | forwards and backs in rugby", "A rugby union team has **forwards** (numbers 1 to 8: props, hooker, locks, flankers and number 8), who win the ball in scrums, lineouts and rucks, and **backs** (9 to 15: scrum-half, fly-half, centers, wings and fullback), who are faster and run and kick the ball."],
  ["what is tag rugby | touch rugby rules", "**Tag rugby** and **touch rugby** are non-contact versions. Instead of tackling, you pull a tag off a belt or touch the ball carrier. They're great for beginners and kids."],
  ["what is lacrosse | how do you play lacrosse | lacrosse rules", "In **lacrosse**, players use a stick with a net pocket to catch, carry and throw a rubber ball, trying to shoot it into the other team's goal. Field lacrosse has 10 players per side in men's play and 12 in women's. Lacrosse comes from games played by Indigenous peoples of North America."],
  ["what is cradling in lacrosse | lacrosse positions", "**Cradling** is rocking the stick back and forth to keep the ball in the pocket while running. Lacrosse positions include **attackers**, **midfielders** (middies), **defenders** and the **goalie**."],
  ["what is box lacrosse | men's vs women's lacrosse", "**Box lacrosse** is played indoors in a hockey-style rink with fewer players and a shot clock. Women's lacrosse limits body contact and uses sticks with shallower pockets, while men's field lacrosse allows body checking and players wear more protective gear."],
  ["what is a penalty corner in field hockey | field hockey rules stick", "In **field hockey** you can only hit the ball with the **flat side** of the stick, and only the goalkeeper can use their body to play it. A **penalty corner** is awarded for certain defensive fouls: the attackers take a pass from the backline and try to score while five defenders rush out."],
  ["what is the shooting circle in field hockey | can you score from anywhere in field hockey", "In field hockey, a goal only counts if the attacker touches the ball inside the **shooting circle** (the D-shaped area in front of the goal). Shots from outside it don't count."],
  ["what is handball | how do you play handball | team handball rules", "**Handball** is a fast team sport with 7 players per side (6 court players and a goalkeeper). Players pass and dribble a ball with their hands and throw it into the goal. Nobody but the goalkeeper can enter the goal area, though players can jump in from outside and shoot before landing."],
  ["handball rules steps | how many steps can you take in handball", "In handball you can take up to **three steps** while holding the ball, hold it for up to **three seconds**, and then you must pass, shoot or dribble. Once you stop dribbling, you can't dribble again."],
  ["what is water polo | how do you play water polo | water polo rules", "**Water polo** is played in a pool by two teams of **7 players** (6 field players and a goalie). Players swim, pass and shoot a ball into the goal using one hand only (except the goalie). Field players can't touch the pool bottom, so they tread water with a strong kick called the **eggbeater**."],
  ["what is the eggbeater kick | how do water polo players stay up", "The **eggbeater kick** is a treading-water technique where the legs move in alternating circles, like an eggbeater. It keeps players high and steady in the water, used in water polo and artistic swimming."],
  ["how do you swim freestyle | front crawl technique | how to do front crawl", "For **front crawl** (freestyle): lie flat on your front, alternate long arm pulls, kick small and fast from the hips, and turn your head to the side to breathe as one arm pulls back. Keep your face in the water between breaths."],
  ["how do you swim breaststroke | breaststroke technique", "For **breaststroke**: pull your hands out in a heart shape, lift your head to breathe, then shoot your arms forward while doing a **frog kick** (bend knees, kick out and snap legs together). Glide briefly before the next stroke. The rhythm is pull, breathe, kick, glide."],
  ["how do you swim backstroke | backstroke technique", "For **backstroke**: float on your back with your face up, kick with straight legs and alternate your arms in big windmill circles, entering the water with your little finger first. Keep your head still and hips high."],
  ["how do you swim butterfly | butterfly stroke technique | what is the hardest swimming stroke", "**Butterfly** is often called the hardest stroke. Both arms sweep over the water together while your body moves in a wave, with a **dolphin kick** (both legs together). Lift your head forward to breathe at the end of the arm pull."],
  ["what is an individual medley | what is im in swimming | medley relay order", "An **individual medley** (IM) is when one swimmer does all four strokes in this order: **butterfly, backstroke, breaststroke, freestyle**. In a **medley relay**, four swimmers each do one stroke, in the order backstroke, breaststroke, butterfly, freestyle."],
  ["what is a flip turn | how do swimmers turn | what is a tumble turn", "A **flip turn** (tumble turn) is a quick somersault at the wall in freestyle and backstroke: you flip, plant your feet on the wall and push off. In breaststroke and butterfly, swimmers must touch the wall with both hands before turning."],
  ["how long is an olympic pool | olympic swimming pool size", "An Olympic-size swimming pool is **50 meters** long and 25 meters wide, with 8 or 10 lanes. Races are also held in **short course** pools that are 25 meters long."],
  ["what is open water swimming | what is artistic swimming", "**Open water swimming** is racing in lakes, rivers or the sea, often over 10 km. **Artistic swimming** (formerly synchronized swimming) combines swimming, dance and gymnastics, with athletes performing routines to music."],
  ["what is diving | diving events | springboard vs platform diving", "In competitive **diving**, athletes jump from a board, doing flips and twists before entering the water. **Springboard** diving uses a flexible board 1 or 3 meters high. **Platform** diving uses a fixed tower, up to 10 meters. Judges score the takeoff, flight and entry, ideally with almost no splash."],
  ["what are track and field events | athletics events list | what sports are in track and field", "Track and field (athletics) includes:\n- **Sprints**: 100 m, 200 m, 400 m\n- **Middle and long distance**: 800 m to 10,000 m\n- **Hurdles** and **steeplechase**\n- **Relays**: 4x100 m, 4x400 m\n- **Jumps**: long jump, triple jump, high jump, pole vault\n- **Throws**: shot put, discus, javelin, hammer\n- **Combined events**: decathlon and heptathlon"],
  ["what is a decathlon | what is a heptathlon", "The **decathlon** is a two-day event with **10** track and field events (like sprinting, jumping and throwing), usually for men. The **heptathlon** has **7** events and is usually for women. Points are scored in each event, and the highest total wins."],
  ["what is the triple jump | how does the triple jump work", "The **triple jump** (once called hop, step and jump) has three phases after a running start: a **hop** (land on the same foot), a **step** (land on the other foot) and a **jump** into the sand pit."],
  ["what is the high jump | what is the fosbury flop", "In the **high jump**, athletes run up and leap over a horizontal bar without knocking it off. Most use the **Fosbury Flop**, going over head-first and backward with the back arched, then landing on a soft mat."],
  ["what is pole vault | how does pole vault work", "In **pole vault**, athletes sprint holding a long, flexible pole, plant it in a box, and let it bend and spring them up over a very high bar. They land on a big soft mat."],
  ["what is shot put | what is discus | what is javelin | what is hammer throw", "The four throws: **shot put** (pushing a heavy metal ball from the shoulder), **discus** (spinning and throwing a flat disc), **javelin** (running and throwing a spear) and **hammer throw** (spinning and releasing a metal ball on a wire). The longest legal throw wins."],
  ["how does a relay race work | what is a baton pass | 4x100 relay", "In a **relay**, a team of four runners each runs one leg and passes a **baton** to the next runner. The pass must happen inside a marked **exchange zone**, and dropping the baton costs huge time."],
  ["what is a false start | why do sprinters use starting blocks", "Sprinters use **starting blocks** to push off powerfully at the gun. A **false start** is moving before the starting gun; in top-level athletics, a single false start usually means disqualification."],
  ["what is hurdles | how high are hurdles | what is steeplechase", "In **hurdles** races, runners jump over barriers placed along the track, with distances like 100 m, 110 m and 400 m. The **steeplechase** (usually 3,000 m) has big barriers and a water jump."],
  ["how do i get faster at running | how to run faster | sprinting tips", "To get faster: practice short sprints with full rest, work on good form (tall posture, driving knees, arms swinging forward and back), do strength exercises like squats and lunges, and include drills like high knees and skips. Rest matters too, since muscles get stronger during recovery."],
  ["what are the gymnastics events | artistic gymnastics apparatus | gymnastics events men and women", "In artistic gymnastics, women compete on **vault, uneven bars, balance beam and floor exercise** (4 events). Men compete on **floor exercise, pommel horse, still rings, vault, parallel bars and horizontal (high) bar** (6 events)."],
  ["what is rhythmic gymnastics | what is trampoline gymnastics", "**Rhythmic gymnastics** combines dance, flexibility and handling equipment like a **ribbon, hoop, ball, clubs or rope** to music. **Trampoline gymnastics** has athletes performing high flips and twists on a trampoline."],
  ["how is gymnastics scored | gymnastics scoring explained", "Modern gymnastics scores have two parts: a **difficulty score** (how hard the skills are, open-ended) and an **execution score** (out of 10, with deductions for mistakes like bent legs, wobbles or falls). They're added together for the final score."],
  ["what is a pommel horse | what are still rings", "The **pommel horse** has two handles on top; gymnasts swing their legs in circles while supporting themselves only with their hands. On the **still rings**, gymnasts hang from two rings and hold incredibly strong positions, trying to keep the rings from swinging."],
  ["how do i do a cartwheel | how to do a handstand | beginner gymnastics skills", "For a **cartwheel**: stand sideways in a lunge, reach down with one hand then the other, kick your legs over in a wheel shape, and land one foot at a time. For a **handstand**, practice kicking up against a wall first with straight arms and a tight core. Always practice on a soft surface!"],
  ["what are the jumps in figure skating | figure skating jumps names | what is an axel", "The six main figure skating jumps are the **toe loop, Salchow, loop, flip, Lutz and Axel**. The **Axel** is the only one that takes off going forward, so it has an extra half rotation; a triple Axel is three and a half spins!"],
  ["what is the difference between a lutz and a flip | toe jumps vs edge jumps", "**Toe jumps** (toe loop, flip, Lutz) use the toe pick of the other skate to vault into the air. **Edge jumps** (Salchow, loop, Axel) take off from the edge of the blade. The **flip** takes off from an inside edge and the **Lutz** from an outside edge, both going backward."],
  ["what are figure skating spins | what is a camel spin | what is a sit spin", "Main spins: the **upright spin** (standing tall), **sit spin** (crouched low on one leg) and **camel spin** (leaning forward with the free leg stretched back, like a T). The **Biellmann spin** pulls the free leg up over the head from behind."],
  ["what is ice dancing | pairs skating vs ice dance", "**Pairs skating** includes big throws, lifts and side-by-side jumps. **Ice dancing** focuses on rhythm, footwork and partnership, like ballroom dancing on ice, with no big jumps or overhead throws."],
  ["what is speed skating | short track vs long track speed skating", "**Long track** speed skating is on a 400 m oval where two skaters race against the clock in separate lanes. **Short track** is on a smaller 111 m track with packs of skaters racing each other, lots of tight turns and passing."],
  ["what are the types of skiing | alpine vs nordic skiing | ski events", "**Alpine** skiing is racing downhill through gates: downhill, super-G, giant slalom and slalom. **Nordic** skiing includes **cross-country** (skiing across flat and hilly terrain) and **ski jumping**. **Freestyle** skiing covers moguls, aerials and halfpipe tricks."],
  ["slalom vs giant slalom | what is slalom skiing | what is downhill skiing", "**Slalom** has gates set close together for quick, tight turns. **Giant slalom** has gates farther apart with wider, faster turns. **Downhill** is the fastest event, with few turns and skiers reaching very high speeds."],
  ["how do i learn to ski | beginner skiing tips | what is a pizza in skiing", "Take a lesson on a gentle beginner slope. Learn the **snowplow** (\"pizza\") by pointing your ski tips together to slow down and stop, then progress to parallel turns (\"french fries\") as you get comfortable. Always wear a helmet and lean slightly forward."],
  ["what are ski slope colors | green blue black ski runs | what does black diamond mean", "In North America, slopes are marked **green circle** (easiest), **blue square** (intermediate), **black diamond** (advanced) and **double black diamond** (experts only). In Europe the colors are usually green or blue, red and black."],
  ["what is snowboarding | regular vs goofy stance | snowboard stance", "**Snowboarding** means riding down snowy slopes on a single board with both feet attached. **Regular** stance means your left foot is in front; **goofy** means your right foot leads. To find yours, think of which foot you'd put forward sliding on a floor in socks."],
  ["snowboarding events | what is halfpipe | what is slopestyle | what is big air", "Snowboarding events include **halfpipe** (tricks on the walls of a U-shaped channel), **slopestyle** (jumps and rails on a course), **big air** (one giant jump with a big trick), **snowboard cross** (racing in a group over bumps and turns) and **parallel giant slalom**."],
  ["what is biathlon | what is nordic combined", "**Biathlon** combines cross-country skiing with **rifle shooting** at targets; missed shots mean penalty loops or time. **Nordic combined** mixes ski jumping and cross-country skiing."],
  ["what is bobsled | what is luge | what is skeleton", "These are sliding sports on an icy track. **Bobsled**: teams of 2 or 4 ride in a steerable sled. **Luge**: athletes lie on their back, feet first. **Skeleton**: athletes slide head-first on their stomach on a small sled."],
  ["how do you surf | how to learn surfing | surfing for beginners", "To start surfing: take a lesson, use a big soft-top board, and practice on small, gentle waves. Lie on the board and paddle, then when a wave lifts you, push up and **pop up** to your feet in one smooth move, staying low with knees bent. Learn about rip currents and always surf with others."],
  ["surfing terms | what is a barrel in surfing | what is hang ten", "Some surf words: **barrel** (or tube) is riding inside the curl of a hollow wave; **hang ten** means standing with all ten toes over the nose of a longboard; **wipeout** is falling off; **line-up** is where surfers wait for waves; **dropping in** is stealing a wave someone else is already riding (bad manners!)."],
  ["shortboard vs longboard surfing | types of surfboards", "**Longboards** (over about 9 feet) are stable and easy to catch waves on, great for beginners and smooth, stylish riding. **Shortboards** are smaller and very maneuverable for sharp turns and tricks on steeper waves. **Fish** and **funboards** are in between."],
  ["what is bodyboarding | what is stand up paddleboarding | what is sup", "**Bodyboarding** is riding waves lying on a short foam board, often with swim fins. **Stand-up paddleboarding** (SUP) means standing on a big board and paddling with a long paddle, on flat water or waves."],
  ["what is an ollie | how do you ollie on a skateboard", "An **ollie** is the basic skateboard jump. Put your back foot on the tail and front foot near the middle, snap the tail down, then slide your front foot up toward the nose to level the board in the air. Bend your knees to land. Most tricks build on it."],
  ["skateboard tricks list | what is a kickflip | what is a shove it", "Classic skateboard tricks:\n- **Ollie**: the basic jump\n- **Kickflip**: an ollie where you flick the board so it spins along its length\n- **Heelflip**: like a kickflip, but flicked with the heel\n- **Pop shove-it**: the board spins 180 degrees flat under you\n- **Manual**: balancing on the back wheels while rolling\n- **Grind**: sliding along a rail or edge on the trucks"],
  ["what is a grind in skateboarding | grind vs slide | what is a 50 50", "A **grind** is scraping along a rail or ledge on the **trucks** (metal axles). A **50-50** grind uses both trucks equally. A **slide** uses the wooden deck instead of the trucks, like a boardslide."],
  ["skateboard stance | regular vs goofy skateboarding | what is switch", "**Regular** skaters ride with the left foot forward, **goofy** skaters with the right foot forward. Riding with your non-natural stance is called **switch**, and **fakie** means rolling backward in your normal stance."],
  ["what are the parts of a skateboard | skateboard parts", "A skateboard has a **deck** (the wooden board), **grip tape** on top for traction, two **trucks** (the metal parts that turn), **wheels** and **bearings**. The front end is the **nose** and the back is the **tail**."],
  ["skateboarding safety | how to fall on a skateboard", "Wear a helmet plus knee, elbow and wrist pads. When you fall, try to **roll** onto the meaty parts of your body instead of catching yourself with straight arms. Start on smooth, flat ground away from traffic."],
  ["what is bmx | bmx racing vs bmx freestyle", "**BMX** stands for **bicycle motocross**. **BMX racing** has riders sprinting around a dirt track with jumps and banked turns. **BMX freestyle** is all about tricks in skateparks, on dirt jumps, on flat ground or on street obstacles."],
  ["bmx tricks | what is a bunny hop | what is a tailwhip", "Popular BMX tricks: the **bunny hop** (lifting both wheels off the ground), **manual** (riding on the back wheel without pedaling), **barspin** (spinning the handlebars in the air), **tailwhip** (spinning the bike frame around the handlebars) and the **360** (spinning a full circle in the air)."],
  ["what is the tour de france | tour de france explained | how does the tour de france work", "The **Tour de France** is a famous multi-week cycling race held mostly in France every summer, split into about **21 daily stages** across flat roads, hills and huge mountain climbs. The rider with the lowest total time wears the leader's **yellow jersey**."],
  ["tour de france jerseys | what is the yellow jersey | what is the polka dot jersey", "Tour de France jerseys:\n- **Yellow**: overall leader (lowest total time)\n- **Green**: points leader, usually the best sprinter\n- **Polka dot** (white with red dots): king of the mountains, best climber\n- **White**: best young rider"],
  ["what is a peloton | what is drafting in cycling | what is a breakaway", "The **peloton** is the main pack of riders in a road race. Riders **draft** behind each other because the rider in front blocks the wind, saving a lot of energy for those behind. A **breakaway** is a small group that escapes ahead of the peloton."],
  ["types of cycling races | track cycling events | what is a velodrome", "Cycling disciplines include **road racing**, **mountain biking**, **BMX**, **cyclocross** and **track cycling**. Track races happen in a **velodrome**, an oval track with steeply banked corners, on bikes with fixed gears and no brakes."],
  ["what is a keirin | what is a madison in cycling", "A **keirin** is a track race where riders follow a pace bike that gradually speeds up and then pulls off, leaving them to sprint the final laps. A **madison** is a team race where two riders take turns, \"hand-slinging\" each other into the race."],
  ["how does boxing work | boxing rules | how do you win a boxing match", "In boxing, two fighters punch each other with padded gloves, only above the belt and only with closed fists. Bouts are split into rounds (often 3 minutes each). You win by **knockout** (opponent can't get up within a 10 count), **technical knockout** (referee stops the fight) or on the judges' **points**."],
  ["boxing punches | what is a jab | what is an uppercut", "The four basic boxing punches: the **jab** (quick straight punch with the front hand), the **cross** (powerful straight punch with the rear hand), the **hook** (a curved punch from the side) and the **uppercut** (an upward punch from below)."],
  ["what are boxing weight classes | why are there weight classes", "Boxers compete in **weight classes**, from light divisions like flyweight up to heavyweight, so fighters of similar size face each other. This makes fights fairer and safer. Wrestling, judo and other combat sports use weight classes too."],
  ["what is karate | karate belts | karate explained", "**Karate** is a martial art from Okinawa, Japan, using punches, kicks, knee and elbow strikes and blocks. Students practice **kata** (set patterns of moves) and **kumite** (sparring). Ranks are shown by colored belts, usually starting at white and reaching **black belt**, which has its own levels called dan."],
  ["what is judo | judo explained | how do you win in judo", "**Judo** is a Japanese martial art meaning \"the gentle way.\" Instead of striking, players use throws, pins, joint locks and chokes. A perfect throw that lands the opponent largely on their back scores **ippon**, which wins the match instantly. A smaller score is **waza-ari**."],
  ["what is taekwondo | taekwondo explained | taekwondo vs karate", "**Taekwondo** is a Korean martial art famous for high, fast and spinning **kicks**. In Olympic sparring, athletes wear electronic body protectors and score more points for kicks to the head and spinning kicks. Karate uses more hand techniques, while taekwondo focuses heavily on kicking."],
  ["what is kung fu | what is wushu | kung fu styles", "**Kung fu** is a general name for the many Chinese martial arts, with hundreds of styles, some inspired by animals like the tiger, crane and mantis. Shaolin and Wing Chun are well-known styles. **Wushu** is the modern sport form, with performances of routines and a sparring part called sanda."],
  ["what is jiu jitsu | what is brazilian jiu jitsu | what is bjj", "**Brazilian jiu-jitsu** (BJJ) is a grappling martial art focused on ground fighting. Using leverage and technique instead of strength, you try to control your opponent and win by a **submission** (like a joint lock or choke, where they \"tap out\") or on points. It grew from Japanese jujutsu and judo."],
  ["what is tai chi | what is aikido | what is muay thai", "**Tai chi** is a Chinese martial art practiced mostly as slow, flowing movements for balance, health and calm. **Aikido** is a Japanese art that redirects an attacker's energy using throws and joint locks. **Muay Thai** is Thailand's striking art, called \"the art of eight limbs\" because it uses fists, elbows, knees and shins."],
  ["what is mma | what is mixed martial arts", "**MMA** (mixed martial arts) is a combat sport that combines striking (like boxing and kickboxing) with grappling (like wrestling and jiu-jitsu). Fighters can win by knockout, submission or judges' decision."],
  ["what is fencing | fencing weapons | foil epee sabre", "**Fencing** is a sport of sword fighting with three weapons:\n- **Foil**: score by hitting the torso with the tip\n- **Épée**: score by hitting anywhere on the body with the tip, no right-of-way\n- **Sabre**: score with the tip or edge, anywhere above the waist\nTouches are detected electronically."],
  ["what is right of way in fencing | how is fencing scored", "In foil and sabre, **right of way** decides who gets the point when both fencers hit at the same time: usually the fencer who started the attack first. Bouts are typically fought to **15 touches** in elimination rounds."],
  ["how does wrestling work | olympic wrestling styles | freestyle vs greco roman", "Olympic wrestling has two styles. In **freestyle**, wrestlers can attack the legs and use their legs for takedowns. In **Greco-Roman**, you can't attack below the waist or use your legs to trip. You win by a **pin (fall)**, by building a big points lead or on points."],
  ["what is a pin in wrestling | how do you score points in wrestling", "A **pin** (or fall) is holding both of your opponent's shoulders on the mat for a moment, which wins the match instantly. Points come from **takedowns**, **escapes**, **reversals** and exposing the opponent's back to the mat."],
  ["what is sumo wrestling | sumo rules", "**Sumo** is a traditional Japanese wrestling sport. Two wrestlers (rikishi) face off in a circular ring. You win by pushing your opponent out of the ring or making any part of their body other than the soles of their feet touch the ground. Bouts are often over in seconds."],
  ["how does archery work | archery scoring | how is archery scored", "In target archery, archers shoot arrows at a target with **10 rings**. The center (gold) scores **10**, and points drop toward the outer rings. Olympic archers shoot from **70 meters** with a **recurve bow**."],
  ["recurve vs compound bow | what is a longbow | types of bows", "A **recurve bow** has limb tips that curve away from the archer for more power. A **compound bow** uses cables and pulleys (cams) to make holding the drawn string easier and more accurate. A **longbow** is a tall, simple bow with straight limbs."],
  ["how is bowling scored | bowling scoring explained | what is a strike in bowling", "A game of bowling has **10 frames**, with up to two rolls per frame to knock down **10 pins**. A **strike** (all pins on the first roll) scores 10 plus the pins from your next two rolls. A **spare** (all pins in two rolls) scores 10 plus your next roll. A perfect game, all strikes, is **300**."],
  ["what is a turkey in bowling | bowling terms | what is a gutter ball", "Bowling slang: a **turkey** is three strikes in a row; a **gutter ball** rolls into the gutter and hits nothing; a **split** is when pins are left standing with a gap between them; a **double** is two strikes in a row. The 10th frame gives bonus rolls if you get a strike or spare."],
  ["what is candlepin bowling | what is duckpin bowling | 10 pin vs 5 pin", "Besides ten-pin bowling, there's **candlepin** and **duckpin** (small balls without finger holes and thinner or shorter pins, three rolls per frame) and **five-pin bowling** (popular in Canada). Each has its own scoring twist."],
  ["how do you play darts | darts rules | how does 501 work", "In the classic darts game **501**, each player starts at 501 and subtracts the points from each throw of three darts. You must finish by landing exactly on zero, with the final dart hitting a **double** (the narrow outer ring). If you go below zero or leave 1, it's a **bust**."],
  ["how is a dartboard scored | what is the bullseye worth | what is the treble ring", "A dartboard has 20 numbered sections. The thin outer ring is **double** and the thin inner ring is **treble** (triple). The outer bull is worth **25** and the inner bullseye **50**. The highest score with three darts is **180** (three treble 20s)."],
  ["how do you play pool | 8 ball rules | how to play billiards", "In **8-ball**, one player takes solids (1-7) and the other stripes (9-15). Use the cue ball to pocket all of your group, then sink the **8 ball** in a pocket you call to win. Pocketing the 8 ball early or scratching on it loses the game."],
  ["what is 9 ball | what is snooker | pool vs snooker", "In **9-ball**, you must always hit the lowest-numbered ball first, and whoever pockets the 9 wins. **Snooker** uses a larger table, 15 reds and six colors: you alternate potting a red and a color, each worth points, then clear the colors in order."],
  ["what is a scratch in pool | what is a foul in billiards", "A **scratch** is when the cue ball goes into a pocket. Fouls also include failing to hit any ball or not hitting your own group first. After a foul, the opponent usually gets **ball in hand**, meaning they can place the cue ball anywhere."],
  ["what is curling | how do you play curling | curling rules", "**Curling** is played on ice by two teams of four. Players slide heavy granite **stones** toward a target called the **house**, while teammates **sweep** the ice in front to help the stone travel farther and straighter. After all stones are thrown in an **end**, the team with the stone closest to the center scores."],
  ["why do they sweep in curling | what is the button in curling | what is the hammer in curling", "Sweeping slightly warms and smooths the ice, which helps the stone slide farther and curl less. The **button** is the center of the house. The **hammer** is the advantage of throwing the last stone in an end."],
  ["what is equestrian | equestrian events | horse riding sports", "Equestrian sports involve horse and rider together. The Olympic events are **dressage** (precise, dance-like movements), **show jumping** (jumping a course of fences without knocking them down) and **eventing** (a combination of dressage, cross-country jumping and show jumping)."],
  ["what is dressage | what is show jumping | what is eventing", "**Dressage** is sometimes called horse ballet: the horse performs precise movements guided by subtle rider signals. **Show jumping** is clearing colorful fences in an arena, with penalties for knockdowns and refusals. **Eventing** is a triathlon for horses combining dressage, cross-country and show jumping."],
  ["horse riding tips for beginners | how to ride a horse | horse gaits", "Start with lessons and a calm horse, always wear a riding helmet, sit tall with your heels down, and hold the reins gently. The main gaits from slowest to fastest are **walk, trot, canter and gallop**."],
  ["what is rowing | rowing vs canoeing vs kayaking | sculling vs sweep rowing", "In **rowing**, athletes sit facing backward and use oars attached to the boat. In **sculling**, each rower has two oars; in **sweep** rowing, each rower has one. In **canoeing** and **kayaking**, paddlers face forward and use paddles held in their hands: a single-bladed paddle for canoes and a double-bladed one for kayaks."],
  ["what is a coxswain | what does the cox do in rowing", "The **coxswain** (cox) sits in the boat, usually at the back, facing the rowers. They steer, call out commands and keep the crew rowing in rhythm. They don't row themselves."],
  ["how long is an ironman | triathlon distances | sprint triathlon distance", "Common triathlon distances:\n- **Sprint**: 750 m swim, 20 km bike, 5 km run\n- **Olympic**: 1.5 km swim, 40 km bike, 10 km run\n- **Half Ironman** (70.3): 1.9 km swim, 90 km bike, 21.1 km run\n- **Ironman**: 3.8 km swim, 180 km bike, 42.2 km run"],
  ["what is a transition in triathlon | what is t1 and t2", "In a triathlon, **transitions** are the changeovers between sports. **T1** is swim to bike, and **T2** is bike to run. The time spent changing counts toward your total, so athletes practice them to be quick."],
  ["how many miles is a marathon | why is a marathon 26.2 miles | marathon history", "A **marathon** is **42.195 km (26.2 miles)**. It's named after the legend of a Greek messenger who ran from the town of Marathon to Athens. The exact distance was fixed in 1921, based on the course used at the 1908 London Olympics."],
  ["what is a half marathon | how long is a 5k | race distances", "Popular running race distances: **5K** (5 km, about 3.1 miles), **10K** (about 6.2 miles), **half marathon** (21.1 km, 13.1 miles) and **marathon** (42.2 km, 26.2 miles). An **ultramarathon** is anything longer than a marathon."],
  ["how do i train for a marathon | marathon training tips | how to train for a 5k", "Build up slowly: run a few times a week and increase your longest run gradually (a common rule is no more than about 10% more each week). Mix easy runs with some faster ones, rest often, eat well, stay hydrated and get good running shoes. Beginners often start with a 5K plan before tackling longer races."],
  ["what is hitting the wall in a marathon | what is runner's high", "**Hitting the wall** is when a long-distance runner suddenly feels exhausted, often around 20 miles, because the body has used up its stored energy (glycogen). Eating and drinking during the race helps. A **runner's high** is the happy, relaxed feeling some people get during or after running."],
  ["what is orienteering | what is parkour", "**Orienteering** is racing through unfamiliar terrain using a map and compass to find checkpoints. **Parkour** is moving through an environment efficiently by running, jumping, climbing and vaulting over obstacles."],
  ["what is rock climbing | bouldering vs sport climbing | climbing types", "**Bouldering** is climbing short walls without ropes, over thick crash pads. **Sport climbing** uses ropes and fixed bolts on taller walls, and **speed climbing** is racing up a standard route. Climbing routes are graded by difficulty."],
  ["when did the olympics start | olympic history | ancient olympics", "The **ancient Olympic Games** began in **Olympia, Greece**, traditionally dated to **776 BC**, and were held every four years in honor of Zeus for over a thousand years. Events included running, wrestling, boxing, chariot racing and the pentathlon."],
  ["when were the modern olympics first held | who started the modern olympics | first modern olympics", "The first modern Olympics were held in **Athens, Greece, in 1896**. They were revived by a Frenchman, **Pierre de Coubertin**, who founded the International Olympic Committee (IOC) in 1894. The first **Winter Olympics** took place in Chamonix, France, in 1924."],
  ["what colors are the olympic rings | olympic rings colors", "The five Olympic rings are **blue, yellow, black, green and red**, on a white background. Pierre de Coubertin designed them in 1913, and at least one of these six colors (including white) appears in every nation's flag."],
  ["what is the olympic motto | olympic motto meaning | citius altius fortius", "The Olympic motto is **\"Citius, Altius, Fortius\"**, Latin for **\"Faster, Higher, Stronger.\"** In 2021 the IOC added the word \"Communiter,\" meaning **\"Together.\"**"],
  ["what is the olympic oath | olympic creed", "At the opening ceremony, an athlete, a judge and a coach take the **Olympic oath**, promising to compete fairly and respect the rules. The Olympic creed says the most important thing is not to win but to **take part**, not the triumph but the struggle."],
  ["what happens at the olympic opening ceremony | parade of nations", "The opening ceremony features a big show by the host country, then the **parade of nations**, where athletes march in behind their flags. Greece traditionally enters first and the host nation last. The Olympic flame is then used to light the **cauldron**, which burns until the closing ceremony."],
  ["what medals do you get at the olympics | gold silver bronze | are olympic gold medals made of gold", "First place wins **gold**, second **silver** and third **bronze**. Modern Olympic gold medals are actually mostly **silver**, plated with at least 6 grams of real gold."],
  ["what is the olympic village | where do olympic athletes stay", "The **Olympic Village** is a special neighborhood built or set up in the host city where athletes from all countries live during the Games. It has dining halls, gyms and shops, and it's a great place for athletes to meet people from around the world."],
  ["what was the first sport in the olympics | what was the stadion race", "The earliest known event at the ancient Olympics was the **stadion**, a sprint of about 190 meters, the length of the stadium. For several Games it was the only event."],
  ["what is the olympic truce | olympic truce meaning", "The **Olympic truce** is an ancient Greek tradition where wars were paused so athletes and fans could travel safely to the Games. Today the United Nations encourages a truce around each Olympics to promote peace."],
  ["what is the youth olympics | youth olympic games", "The **Youth Olympic Games** are for athletes aged 15 to 18. They have summer and winter editions, include some new and mixed-team events, and focus on education and cultural exchange as well as sport."],
  ["when did the paralympics start | history of the paralympics | stoke mandeville games", "The Paralympics grew from the **Stoke Mandeville Games**, organized in England in 1948 by Dr. Ludwig Guttmann for injured World War II veterans. The first official **Paralympic Games** were held in **Rome in 1960**. The name means \"parallel\" to the Olympics."],
  ["what is the paralympic symbol | what are the agitos", "The Paralympic symbol is three **agitos**, crescent shapes in **red, blue and green**. \"Agito\" is Latin for \"I move,\" and the symbol represents movement and athletes always striving forward."],
  ["paralympic sports | wheelchair basketball | what is goalball | what is boccia", "Paralympic sports include **wheelchair basketball**, **wheelchair rugby**, **para swimming**, **para athletics**, **sitting volleyball** and more. **Goalball** is for athletes with visual impairments, who roll a ball with bells inside toward the other team's goal. **Boccia** is a precision ball game similar to bocce."],
  ["how are paralympic athletes classified | paralympic classification", "Paralympic athletes are grouped into **classes** based on how their impairment affects their sport, so competition is fair. Athletes with visual impairments may race with a sighted **guide** who runs or skis beside them."],
  ["what are the special olympics | special olympics vs paralympics", "The **Special Olympics** is a sports organization for people with **intellectual disabilities**, offering year-round training and competitions, with a focus on inclusion and every athlete's effort. The **Paralympics** are elite international Games for athletes with a range of physical, visual and intellectual impairments."],
  ["what is the world cup | how does the world cup work | fifa world cup explained", "The **FIFA World Cup** is the biggest soccer tournament in the world, held **every four years**. National teams play qualifying matches for a spot in the finals, then compete in a group stage followed by knockout rounds until one team wins the trophy."],
  ["when was the first world cup | world cup history", "The first FIFA World Cup was held in **Uruguay in 1930**. Since then it's been played every four years, except for 1942 and 1946, which were cancelled because of World War II. There's also a **Women's World Cup**, first held in 1991."],
  ["what is the group stage | what is a knockout round | how do tournaments work", "In a **group stage**, teams are put into small groups and each plays the others; usually 3 points for a win and 1 for a draw, and the top teams advance. In the **knockout stage**, every match must have a winner, and the loser goes home, through the round of 16, quarterfinals, semifinals and the final."],
  ["what is a round robin tournament | what is a single elimination bracket", "In a **round robin**, every team plays every other team, and the best record wins. In **single elimination**, you're out after one loss, so the bracket gets halved each round. **Double elimination** lets you lose once and still have a chance through a losers' bracket."],
  ["what is a world series | what is the stanley cup | what is the nba finals", "These are the championship series of major North American leagues: the **World Series** for baseball (MLB), the **Stanley Cup Final** for ice hockey (NHL) and the **NBA Finals** for basketball. Each is a best-of-seven series between the top teams."],
  ["what is a best of seven series | best of 7 meaning", "A **best-of-seven** series means two teams play up to seven games, and the first to win **four** games wins the series. It's used in many playoffs because it's fairer than a single game."],
  ["what does mvp mean | what is an mvp | most valuable player", "**MVP** stands for **most valuable player**, an award given to the player who did the most to help their team in a game, series or season."],
  ["what is overtime in sports | what is sudden death | what is a golden goal", "**Overtime** is extra playing time when a game is tied at the end. **Sudden death** means the first team to score wins immediately. The **golden goal** was a sudden-death rule once used in soccer extra time."],
  ["what does home team and away team mean | what is home advantage", "The **home team** plays at its own stadium, and the **away team** travels there. **Home advantage** is the boost teams often get at home from supportive fans, a familiar field and no travel."],
  ["what is a referee | what does an umpire do | referee vs umpire", "A **referee** or **umpire** is the official who enforces the rules, makes calls and keeps the game fair. Which name is used depends on the sport: soccer, basketball and hockey have referees, while baseball, cricket and tennis have umpires."],
  ["what does a coach do | what is a captain in sports", "A **coach** teaches skills, plans training and strategies, and makes decisions like substitutions. The **captain** is a player who leads the team on the field, encourages teammates and often talks with the referee."],
  ["what is a draw | what is a tie in sports | what is a stalemate in sports", "A **draw** or **tie** is when a game ends with both sides on the same score. Some sports allow draws (like soccer league matches), while others use overtime, shootouts or tiebreakers to find a winner."],
  ["what is a shutout | what is a comeback in sports", "A **shutout** is when a team stops its opponent from scoring at all. A **comeback** is when a team that was losing by a lot rallies to win, one of the most exciting things in sports!"],
  ["what is ultimate frisbee | how do you play ultimate | ultimate frisbee rules", "In **ultimate**, two teams of seven pass a flying disc and score by catching it in the opponent's end zone. You can't run while holding the disc, and you have about 10 seconds to throw. It's usually **self-refereed**, relying on the \"Spirit of the Game\" for fair play."],
  ["what is netball | how do you play netball | netball rules", "**Netball** is played by two teams of 7 who pass a ball and shoot it through a hoop with no backboard. You can't run or dribble with the ball, must pass within 3 seconds, and each player has a position that limits which areas of the court they can enter. Only two players per team can shoot."],
  ["what is kickball | how do you play kickball", "**Kickball** is like baseball, but the pitcher rolls a big rubber ball and the kicker kicks it. Kickers run the bases, and fielders get outs by catching the ball in the air, tagging runners or throwing to a base. It's a classic playground game."],
  ["what is weightlifting | snatch vs clean and jerk | olympic weightlifting", "Olympic **weightlifting** has two lifts: the **snatch**, lifting the bar from the floor to overhead in one movement, and the **clean and jerk**, lifting it to the shoulders and then driving it overhead. The best total of both wins."],
  ["what is powerlifting | powerlifting lifts", "**Powerlifting** tests strength in three lifts: the **squat**, the **bench press** and the **deadlift**. Each lifter gets three attempts per lift, and the best results are added for a total."],
  ["what is sailing as a sport | how does sailing racing work", "In **sailing** races, boats compete around a course marked by buoys, using wind and skill to go as fast as possible. Sailors must tack (zigzag) to sail toward the wind, and racing rules decide who has right of way when boats meet."],
  ["what is kabaddi | how do you play kabaddi", "**Kabaddi** is a contact team sport from South Asia. One player, the raider, runs into the other team's half, tries to tag defenders and return without being tackled, all while chanting \"kabaddi, kabaddi\" in one breath. Each defender tagged scores a point."],
  ["what is sepak takraw | what is hurling | what is gaelic football", "**Sepak takraw** is from Southeast Asia: like volleyball, but players use their feet, knees, chest and head to get a rattan ball over the net. **Hurling** is an ancient Irish sport using a wooden stick (hurley) and a small ball. **Gaelic football** is an Irish game mixing soccer and rugby skills."],
  ["what is australian rules football | aussie rules | afl rules", "**Australian rules football** is played on a large oval field by teams of 18. Players kick, handball (punch) and run with the ball, bouncing it every 15 meters. Kicking between the two tall middle posts scores a **goal** (6 points), and between a middle and outer post scores a **behind** (1 point)."],
  ["what is polo | how do you play polo | what is a chukker", "**Polo** is played on horseback: two teams of four use long-handled mallets to hit a small ball into the other team's goal. A match is divided into periods called **chukkas** or chukkers, and riders switch horses often."],
  ["what is croquet | what is bocce | what is petanque", "**Croquet** uses mallets to knock balls through hoops (wickets) on a lawn. **Bocce** and **pétanque** are throwing games where you toss balls as close as possible to a small target ball (the pallino or jack). Pétanque is played with metal balls and you throw from a standing circle."],
  ["how do you play cornhole | cornhole rules | bean bag toss", "In **cornhole**, players toss bean bags at a slanted board with a hole. A bag in the hole scores **3 points**, and a bag on the board scores **1**. Points cancel out each round, so only the difference counts, and the first to **21** wins."],
  ["what is horseshoes | how do you play horseshoes", "In **horseshoes**, players toss U-shaped metal shoes at a stake in the ground. A **ringer** (circling the stake) scores 3 points, and the closest shoe within 6 inches scores 1. Games are often played to 21 or 40 points."],
  ["what is cheerleading | is cheerleading a sport", "**Competitive cheerleading** combines tumbling, stunts (lifting and tossing teammates), jumps, dance and cheers into a timed routine judged on difficulty and execution. It takes a lot of strength, teamwork and practice, so many people consider it a sport."],
  ["what is triple jump vs long jump | how does long jump work", "In the **long jump**, athletes sprint down a runway and leap from a takeoff board into a sand pit; the distance is measured to the closest mark they make in the sand. Stepping over the board is a foul. The triple jump adds a hop and a step before the final jump."],
  ["what is the pentathlon | what is modern pentathlon", "The **ancient pentathlon** was running, long jump, discus, javelin and wrestling. The **modern pentathlon**, created by Pierre de Coubertin, was designed around the skills of a soldier: fencing, swimming, horse riding (being replaced by an obstacle course), plus a laser pistol shooting and running combination."],
  ["what is dodgeball strategy | how do i win at dodgeball", "Dodgeball tips: stay alert at the back when you don't have a ball, catch throws aimed at your middle (catching gets the thrower out!), throw at opponents' feet since they're hard to dodge, and team up to throw at the same target at once. Always aim below the shoulders."],
  ["what is a marathon vs sprint | sprinting vs long distance running", "**Sprinting** is short, all-out speed over distances like 100 or 200 meters, using fast-twitch muscles. **Long distance** running is about endurance and pacing over many kilometers. Middle distance (800 m to 1,500 m) needs a mix of both."],
  ["what is a personal best | what does pb mean | what is pr in sports", "A **personal best** (PB), also called a **personal record** (PR), is your best-ever result in an event, like your fastest mile. Chasing your own PB is a great way to stay motivated, no matter how you compare to others."],
  ["how do i warm up before sports | warm up exercises | why should you warm up", "A good warm-up takes 5 to 10 minutes: start with light jogging or jumping jacks to raise your heart rate, then do **dynamic stretches** like leg swings, arm circles, high knees and lunges. Warming up gets blood to your muscles, loosens joints and helps prevent injuries."],
  ["dynamic vs static stretching | when should you stretch | stretching tips", "**Dynamic stretching** uses controlled movements (leg swings, walking lunges) and is best **before** exercise. **Static stretching**, holding a stretch for 20 to 30 seconds, is best **after** exercise when muscles are warm. Never bounce or stretch to the point of pain."],
  ["what stretches should i do after running | cool down exercises | how to cool down", "To cool down, walk or jog slowly for a few minutes, then hold gentle stretches for 20 to 30 seconds: **hamstring stretch** (reach toward your toes), **quad stretch** (pull your heel to your bottom), **calf stretch** against a wall, and a **hip flexor** lunge stretch. Drink some water too."],
  ["how do i get better at a sport | how to improve at soccer | how to practice sports at home", "To improve at any sport:\n- Practice the basics a little every day\n- Focus on one skill at a time and get it right slowly before speeding up\n- Watch skilled players and copy their technique\n- Ask a coach or teammate for feedback\n- Build general fitness: running, balance and strength\n- Rest and sleep well so your body can recover"],
  ["how do i get more stamina | how to build endurance for sports", "Build stamina gradually: do activities like running, cycling or swimming a few times a week, slowly increasing how long you go. Mix in **interval training** (short bursts of hard effort with rest), eat well, drink water and get enough sleep."],
  ["how do i deal with losing a match | how to handle losing | i lost my game and feel sad", "Losing is part of every sport, even for the best athletes. It's okay to feel disappointed. Take a breath, congratulate the winners, and then think about one thing you learned and one thing to practice. Every loss is a lesson that helps you improve."],
  ["what is good sportsmanship | how to be a good sport | examples of good sportsmanship", "Good **sportsmanship** means playing fair and showing respect:\n- Follow the rules and don't cheat\n- Shake hands or say \"good game\" whether you win or lose\n- Respect referees, coaches, teammates and opponents\n- Encourage teammates instead of blaming them\n- Win without bragging and lose without sulking"],
  ["how do i be a good teammate | teamwork in sports", "Be a good teammate by encouraging others, passing the ball, listening to your coach, showing up on time, cheering when teammates do well and helping them up when they fall. Remember: \"there's no I in team!\""],
  ["how do i get over nerves before a game | sports anxiety | how to calm down before a match", "Feeling nervous before a game is normal and shows you care! Try slow deep breaths (in for 4, out for 6), picture yourself doing well, focus on your warm-up routine, and remember to just do your best and have fun."],
  ["how do i prevent sports injuries | sports safety tips", "To stay safe in sports: warm up and cool down, wear the right gear (helmets, shin guards, mouthguards), learn proper technique, drink water, rest when you're tired, and tell an adult if something hurts. Playing through real pain can make injuries worse."],
  ["what should i eat before a game | sports nutrition for kids", "A few hours before playing, eat a balanced meal with carbohydrates (like pasta, rice or bread), some protein and fruit or veggies. A small snack like a banana an hour before helps too. Drink water before, during and after exercise."],
  ["what is a sports drink | water or sports drink", "For most kids and normal activity, **water** is the best drink. Sports drinks contain sugar and salts (electrolytes) and are mainly helpful during long, intense exercise or in very hot weather."],
  ["what is en passant | en passant rule | how does en passant work", "**En passant** (French for \"in passing\") is a special pawn capture. If an enemy pawn moves **two squares** forward from its starting square and lands right beside your pawn, you can capture it as if it had moved only one square, moving your pawn diagonally behind it. You must do it **immediately** on your next move or lose the chance."],
  ["what is pawn promotion | what happens when a pawn reaches the end | can a pawn become a queen", "When a pawn reaches the far side of the board, it **promotes**: you swap it for a queen, rook, bishop or knight of your color. Most players choose a **queen**, so you can even have more than one queen! Choosing something else is called **underpromotion**."],
  ["castling rules | when can you castle | can you castle out of check | kingside vs queenside castling", "You can castle only if: the king and that rook **haven't moved yet**, there are **no pieces between** them, the king is **not in check**, and the king doesn't pass through or land on a square that's attacked. **Kingside** castling (O-O) is the short side; **queenside** (O-O-O) is the long side."],
  ["how does a pawn move | how do pawns capture | pawn rules chess", "A pawn moves **straight forward one square**, or two squares on its very first move. It **captures diagonally** forward one square. Pawns can never move backward, and they're blocked if a piece is directly in front of them."],
  ["how does the bishop move | how does the rook move | how does the queen move", "The **bishop** moves any number of squares diagonally, so it always stays on one color. The **rook** moves any number of squares in straight lines along rows and columns. The **queen** combines both, moving any distance straight or diagonally, which makes her the most powerful piece."],
  ["how does the king move | can the king capture | what is check", "The **king** moves one square in any direction. It can capture, but it can never move into **check** (a square where it could be captured). **Check** means the king is under attack; you must get out by moving the king, blocking the attack or capturing the attacking piece."],
  ["what is stalemate | stalemate vs checkmate | is stalemate a win", "**Stalemate** is when the player to move has **no legal moves** but their king is **not** in check. It's a **draw**, not a win! Checkmate is when the king is in check and there's no way to escape, which wins the game. Be careful not to stalemate your opponent when you're winning."],
  ["how can a chess game end in a draw | draw rules chess | threefold repetition", "A chess game can be a **draw** by: **stalemate**, **agreement** between players, **threefold repetition** (the same position occurring three times), the **50-move rule** (50 moves each with no capture or pawn move), or **insufficient material** (like king versus king)."],
  ["how do you set up a chess board | chess board setup | which way does the chess board go", "Place the board so each player has a **light square in the bottom-right corner** (\"light on the right\"). Rooks go in the corners, then knights, then bishops. The **queen goes on her own color** (white queen on a light square, black queen on a dark square), and the king beside her. Pawns fill the second row. White moves first."],
  ["chess piece values | how many points is a queen worth | what are chess pieces worth", "Common chess piece values:\n- **Pawn**: 1\n- **Knight**: 3\n- **Bishop**: 3\n- **Rook**: 5\n- **Queen**: 9\nThe king is priceless, since losing it loses the game. These numbers help you decide if a trade is good."],
  ["best chess openings for beginners | what is a chess opening | chess opening principles", "Good opening principles: control the **center** with pawns (like e4 or d4), **develop** your knights and bishops quickly, **castle** early to protect your king, and don't move the same piece twice or bring your queen out too soon. Popular beginner openings include the **Italian Game**, **Ruy Lopez** and **Queen's Gambit**."],
  ["what is the italian game | what is the ruy lopez | what is the sicilian defense", "The **Italian Game** (1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4) aims the bishop at Black's weak f7 square. The **Ruy Lopez** (3.Bb5 instead) pressures the knight defending e5. The **Sicilian Defense** (1.e4 c5) is a sharp reply where Black fights for the center from the side."],
  ["what is the queen's gambit | what is a gambit in chess", "A **gambit** is an opening where you offer a pawn (or more) to get faster development or a better position. In the **Queen's Gambit** (1.d4 d5 2.c4), White offers the c-pawn to pull Black's d-pawn away from the center. It's one of the oldest and most respected openings."],
  ["what is scholar's mate | what is fool's mate | fastest checkmate", "**Fool's Mate** is the fastest possible checkmate: just **two moves** by Black after White weakens their king with poor pawn moves. **Scholar's Mate** is a four-move checkmate where the queen and bishop attack the f7 (or f2) square. Defend it by developing a knight to f6 or guarding f7."],
  ["chess tactics | what is a fork in chess | what is a pin in chess | what is a skewer", "Key chess tactics:\n- **Fork**: one piece attacks two or more pieces at once (knights are great at this)\n- **Pin**: a piece can't move without exposing a more valuable piece behind it\n- **Skewer**: like a pin in reverse, a valuable piece is attacked and must move, exposing a piece behind it\n- **Discovered attack**: moving one piece reveals an attack by another"],
  ["how do you read chess notation | algebraic notation | what does nf3 mean", "Chess uses **algebraic notation**: columns are a to h, rows are 1 to 8. Pieces are K (king), Q (queen), R (rook), B (bishop), N (knight), and pawns have no letter. **Nf3** means a knight moves to f3. **x** means a capture, **+** is check, **#** is checkmate, and **O-O** is castling."],
  ["what is a chess clock | what is blitz chess | what is rapid chess", "A **chess clock** has two timers; after you move, you press your button to start your opponent's clock. If your time runs out, you lose. **Blitz** games give each player a few minutes (usually 3 to 5), **rapid** about 10 to 60 minutes, and **bullet** under 3 minutes."],
  ["what is touch move in chess | touch move rule", "The **touch-move** rule says that in serious games, if you deliberately touch one of your pieces, you must move it (if it has a legal move), and if you touch an opponent's piece, you must capture it if you can. To adjust a piece without moving it, say \"I adjust\" first."],
  ["what is a grandmaster in chess | chess ratings | what is elo", "**Grandmaster** (GM) is the highest title a chess player can earn, besides world champion. Players' strength is measured with a **rating** (like the **Elo** system): you gain points by beating others and lose points when you lose."],
  ["what is the endgame in chess | chess endgame tips | middlegame", "Chess has three phases: the **opening** (developing pieces), the **middlegame** (plans and attacks) and the **endgame** (few pieces left). In endgames, activate your **king**, push passed pawns toward promotion and learn basic checkmates like king and queen versus king."],
  ["checkers king rules | what happens when you get kinged | can kings move backwards in checkers", "When a checker reaches the far row, it's **crowned** a king by stacking another piece on top. Kings can move and jump **diagonally backward as well as forward**. In standard American checkers, kings still move only one square at a time unless jumping."],
  ["do you have to jump in checkers | forced capture checkers | multiple jumps checkers", "In standard checkers, if you can jump an opponent's piece, you **must** take the jump. If after jumping you can jump again with the same piece, you keep going in a **multi-jump**. You win by capturing all your opponent's pieces or leaving them with no legal moves."],
  ["what is chinese checkers | how do you play chinese checkers", "**Chinese checkers** is played on a six-pointed star board by 2 to 6 players. Move your marbles one step into an empty hole, or **hop** over any marble into an empty space, chaining hops together. The first to move all their marbles into the opposite point wins. (Despite the name, it was invented in Germany!)"],
  ["monopoly rules | how do you play monopoly | how to win monopoly", "In **Monopoly**, players roll dice, move around the board and buy properties. When others land on your property, they pay **rent**. Owning all properties of one color lets you build **houses and hotels** to raise the rent. Players who run out of money are bankrupt, and the last player left wins."],
  ["what happens on free parking in monopoly | monopoly go to jail rules | how do you get out of jail in monopoly", "By the official rules, **Free Parking** is just a resting space with no money prize (paying out money is a popular house rule). If you go to **jail**, you get out by paying the fine, using a **Get Out of Jail Free** card or rolling doubles within three turns. Rolling doubles three times in a row also sends you to jail!"],
  ["monopoly tips | best monopoly properties | monopoly strategy", "Monopoly strategy tips: buy as much as you can early, aim to complete color sets, build up to **three houses** quickly since that's where rent jumps, and keep some cash for emergencies. The **orange** properties are often considered great because players leaving jail land on them a lot."],
  ["how do you play scrabble | scrabble rules", "In **Scrabble**, each player has **7 letter tiles**. Take turns placing words on the board that connect to words already there, reading left to right or top to bottom. Each letter has a point value, and colored **premium squares** double or triple letters or words. Refill your rack after each turn. The highest score wins."],
  ["what is a bingo in scrabble | scrabble tips | scrabble two letter words", "Using all **7 tiles** in one turn is called a **bingo** (or bonus) and earns **50 extra points**. Tips: learn short words like QI, ZA, XI, JO and AX, use premium squares with high-value letters, keep a good mix of vowels and consonants, and save S and blank tiles for big plays."],
  ["how do you play clue | clue rules | how does cluedo work", "In **Clue** (Cluedo), a murder mystery game, three secret cards (a **suspect**, a **weapon** and a **room**) are hidden in an envelope. Players move around the mansion, make **suggestions** in rooms, and others secretly show a card to disprove them. Use notes to eliminate options, then make an **accusation**. If correct, you win; if wrong, you're out."],
  ["how do you play risk | risk board game rules", "In **Risk**, players try to conquer the world map. Each turn, you place new **armies**, attack neighboring territories by rolling dice (the attacker rolls up to 3 dice, the defender up to 2, highest dice compared) and then move troops. Holding whole continents gives bonus armies. Eliminate everyone or complete your mission to win."],
  ["how do you play battleship | battleship rules | battleship strategy", "In **Battleship**, each player secretly places ships on a grid. Take turns calling coordinates like \"B-4\" to fire. Your opponent says **hit** or **miss**, and when all squares of a ship are hit, it's sunk. Sink all enemy ships first to win. Tip: search in a checkerboard pattern, since every ship covers at least two squares."],
  ["how do you play connect four | connect 4 rules | connect four strategy", "In **Connect Four**, players take turns dropping colored discs into a 7-column, 6-row grid. The first to get **four in a row** horizontally, vertically or diagonally wins. Tips: take the **center column** early, watch for your opponent's threats, and try to set up two threats at once."],
  ["how do you play sorry | sorry board game rules", "In **Sorry!**, each player tries to move all four pawns from Start to Home. You draw a card to see how far to move; a 1 or 2 lets you leave Start. Land on an opponent and you bump them back to Start (\"Sorry!\"). Slides on the board give you a shortcut. The first to get all pawns home wins."],
  ["how do you play uno | uno rules | uno card game", "In **Uno**, each player starts with **7 cards**. Match the top card of the discard pile by **color or number** (or symbol). Special cards: **Skip**, **Reverse**, **Draw Two**, **Wild** (choose a color) and **Wild Draw Four**. When you have one card left, shout **\"Uno!\"** or you have to draw two. First to empty their hand wins the round."],
  ["can you stack draw 2 in uno | uno house rules | uno stacking", "In the official Uno rules, you **can't** stack Draw Two or Draw Four cards: the next player draws and loses their turn. Stacking is a very popular **house rule**, though, so just agree on the rules before you start!"],
  ["how do you play go | go board game rules | what is go game", "**Go** is an ancient strategy game from China played on a 19x19 grid (beginners often use 9x9). Players take turns placing black and white stones on the intersections, trying to surround territory. Stones completely surrounded by the opponent are captured. At the end, the player with more territory plus captures wins."],
  ["what are liberties in go | what is ko in go | go strategy for beginners", "**Liberties** are the empty points directly next to a stone or group. When a group has no liberties, it's captured. The **ko** rule stops players from instantly recapturing back and forth forever. Beginner tips: corners are easiest to claim, and groups with two separate \"eyes\" can never be captured."],
  ["how do you play backgammon | backgammon rules", "In **backgammon**, each player has 15 checkers and moves them around a board of 24 points according to two dice rolls, racing to bring them all home and **bear them off**. A single checker alone on a point (a **blot**) can be hit and sent to the bar, and must re-enter before you move anything else. Rolling doubles means you move four times."],
  ["how do you play mancala | mancala rules", "In **Mancala**, each player has a row of six pits with stones and a store on their right. Pick up all stones from one of your pits and drop one in each pit counterclockwise, including your store. Land in your store and you get another turn. Land in an empty pit on your side and you capture the stones opposite. The most stones in your store at the end wins."],
  ["how do you play dominoes | dominoes rules | block dominoes", "In basic **dominoes**, each player draws a hand of tiles. Take turns matching a tile's end to an open end of the line, with the same number of pips. If you can't play, you pass (or draw in the draw version). The first to play all their tiles wins, or if everyone's blocked, the player with the fewest pips wins."],
  ["what is mexican train dominoes | how many dominoes in a set", "A standard double-six set has **28 dominoes**. **Mexican Train** uses a larger set (often double-twelve): players build their own trains from a central hub plus a shared Mexican train, and must play on doubles before moving on. Lowest total after all rounds wins."],
  ["how do you play jenga | jenga rules | jenga tips", "In **Jenga**, build a tower of 54 wooden blocks in layers of three. Take turns removing one block from anywhere below the top completed layer, **using only one hand**, and placing it on top. Whoever makes the tower fall loses. Tip: gently tap blocks to find loose ones, and middle blocks are often safest."],
  ["how do you play pictionary | pictionary rules", "In **Pictionary**, teams take turns: one player draws a secret word while teammates try to guess it before the timer runs out. No letters, numbers or talking allowed! Correct guesses let your team move forward on the board."],
  ["how do you play charades | charades rules | charades signs", "In **charades**, one person acts out a word or phrase without speaking while their team guesses. Common signs: hold up fingers for the number of words, tap your arm with fingers for syllables, cup your ear for \"sounds like\" and make a circle with your hand for \"the whole thing.\""],
  ["charades ideas | charades words for kids | give me a charades word", "Fun charades ideas: **brushing your teeth**, **riding a bike**, **elephant**, **swimming**, **superhero**, **making a pizza**, **penguin**, **playing the guitar**, **astronaut**, **fishing** and **taking a selfie**."],
  ["how do you play yahtzee | yahtzee rules", "In **Yahtzee**, roll five dice up to three times per turn, keeping the dice you like. Then score the result in one of 13 categories, like three of a kind, full house or a straight. A **Yahtzee** is five of a kind, worth 50 points. Each category can be used only once, so plan carefully!"],
  ["how do you play snakes and ladders | chutes and ladders rules", "In **Snakes and Ladders** (Chutes and Ladders), roll the die and move your piece along the numbered squares. Land at the bottom of a **ladder** and climb up; land on a **snake's head** (or chute) and slide down. The first player to reach the last square wins. It's all luck, so anyone can win!"],
  ["how do you play candy land | candy land rules", "In **Candy Land**, draw a card and move your gingerbread piece to the next space of that color (two spaces on a double-color card). Special picture cards jump you to that spot on the board, forward or back. The first to reach the Candy Castle wins. No reading needed, so it's great for young kids."],
  ["how do you play ludo | ludo rules | how to play parcheesi", "In **Ludo** (similar to **Parcheesi**), each player has four tokens that start in a base. Roll a 6 to bring a token out, then race it around the board to your home column. Landing on an opponent sends them back to base. Rolling a 6 usually gives an extra turn. Get all four tokens home to win."],
  ["how do you play trouble | how do you play guess who", "In **Trouble**, press the Pop-O-Matic dome to roll the die and race your four pegs around the board; landing on others sends them home. In **Guess Who?**, ask yes-or-no questions like \"Does your person wear glasses?\" to flip down faces until you can guess your opponent's mystery person."],
  ["how do you play othello | reversi rules", "In **Othello** (Reversi), players place discs on an 8x8 board, one side black and the other white. When you place a disc so that it traps opponent discs in a straight line between two of yours, they flip to your color. Corners are very valuable because they can never be flipped. Most discs at the end wins."],
  ["how do you play catan | settlers of catan rules", "In **Catan**, players build roads, settlements and cities on an island made of hexagon tiles. Dice rolls produce resources like wood, brick, sheep, wheat and ore, which you trade and spend. Rolling a 7 moves the robber. Reach **10 victory points** first to win."],
  ["how do you play ticket to ride | ticket to ride rules", "In **Ticket to Ride**, players collect colored train cards and use them to claim railway routes between cities on a map. Secret **destination tickets** give bonus points if you connect those cities, but lose points if you don't. The longest continuous route earns a bonus."],
  ["what is a cooperative board game | co op board games", "In a **cooperative** board game, all players work together as a team against the game itself, winning or losing together. It's a great choice for families who don't like arguments, and many puzzle-solving and adventure games work this way."],
  ["how do you play mahjong | what is mahjong", "**Mahjong** is a tile game that started in China, usually played by four people with 144 tiles of suits like bamboo, characters and circles, plus honor tiles. Players draw and discard tiles to form a complete hand of sets (like three of a kind or a run) plus a pair. It has many regional rule variations."],
  ["what is mahjong solitaire | mahjong solitaire vs mahjong", "**Mahjong solitaire** is a one-player puzzle using mahjong tiles stacked in a pattern. You remove **matching pairs** of free tiles (not covered and with a free left or right side) until the board is clear. It's quite different from the four-player game."],
  ["how do you play boggle | boggle rules", "In **Boggle**, shake a grid of letter dice, then set a timer (usually 3 minutes). Find words by connecting letters that touch, including diagonally, without reusing a die in the same word. Words must be at least three letters. Words that other players also found don't score, so unusual words are best!"],
  ["what is dungeons and dragons | how do you play d&d | what is a tabletop rpg", "**Dungeons & Dragons** is a tabletop role-playing game where players create characters and go on adventures told by a **Dungeon Master**, who describes the world. Players say what their characters do, and dice (especially the 20-sided d20) decide whether actions succeed. It's all about storytelling and imagination."],
  ["how do you play apples to apples | how do you play bingo", "In **Apples to Apples**, a judge flips a description card, and everyone else secretly plays the noun card they think fits best; the judge picks the winner. In **Bingo**, a caller announces random numbers, and players mark them on their cards. The first to complete a line (or pattern) shouts \"Bingo!\""],
  ["poker hand rankings | what beats what in poker | poker hands in order", "Poker hands from best to worst:\n1. **Royal flush**: A-K-Q-J-10 of one suit\n2. **Straight flush**: five in a row, same suit\n3. **Four of a kind**\n4. **Full house**: three of a kind plus a pair\n5. **Flush**: five of the same suit\n6. **Straight**: five in a row, mixed suits\n7. **Three of a kind**\n8. **Two pair**\n9. **One pair**\n10. **High card**"],
  ["does a flush beat a straight | does a full house beat a flush", "Yes to both! A **flush** beats a **straight**, and a **full house** beats a flush. The rarer the hand, the higher it ranks. Four of a kind beats a full house, and a straight flush beats everything except a royal flush (which is really the best straight flush)."],
  ["how do you play poker as a game | what is texas hold em | poker rules for fun", "In **Texas Hold'em**, each player gets two private cards, and five shared community cards are dealt face up in stages (the flop, the turn and the river). Players make the best five-card hand from all seven. In friendly games, people often play with chips, candy or counters just for fun."],
  ["how do you play go fish | go fish rules", "In **Go Fish**, deal 5 to 7 cards to each player. On your turn, ask another player for a rank you hold, like \"Do you have any 7s?\" If they do, they hand them over and you go again. If not, they say **\"Go fish!\"** and you draw a card. Collect all four of a rank to make a **book**. The player with the most books wins."],
  ["how do you play war card game | war card game rules | what happens in a war tie", "In **War**, split the deck evenly face down. Both players flip their top card, and the higher card takes both. If the cards tie, it's **war**: each player puts down three cards face down and one face up, and the higher face-up card wins the whole pile. Win by collecting all the cards."],
  ["how do you play crazy eights | crazy eights rules", "In **Crazy Eights**, deal each player 5 to 7 cards and flip one to start the discard pile. Play a card that matches the top card's **suit or rank**. **Eights are wild**: play one anytime and choose the new suit. If you can't play, draw from the pile. First to get rid of all their cards wins. (It's like the ancestor of Uno!)"],
  ["how do you play rummy | rummy rules | what is a meld in rummy", "In **Rummy**, players draw and discard cards trying to form **melds**: **sets** (three or four of the same rank) or **runs** (three or more in a row of the same suit). Lay melds down on the table and add to others' melds. The first player to get rid of all their cards wins the round."],
  ["how do you play gin rummy | what is gin in gin rummy | what is knocking in gin rummy", "In **Gin Rummy**, two players each get 10 cards and draw and discard to form sets and runs. Unmatched cards are **deadwood**. You can **knock** when your deadwood totals 10 or less, or go **gin** with no deadwood at all for a bonus. Points are scored from the difference in deadwood."],
  ["how do you play hearts | hearts card game rules | what is shooting the moon", "In **Hearts**, four players try to **avoid** taking tricks with hearts (1 point each) and the **queen of spades** (13 points). You must follow suit if you can. Hearts can't be led until one has been played. **Shooting the moon** means taking all the hearts plus the queen of spades, which gives everyone else 26 points instead! The lowest score wins."],
  ["how do you play spades | spades card game rules | what is a bid in spades", "**Spades** is a partnership trick-taking game for four. Each player **bids** how many tricks they expect to win, and partners add their bids together. **Spades are always trump**, beating any other suit. Make your bid to score points; fall short and you lose points. Extra tricks are bags, and too many cost points."],
  ["what is a trick in card games | what does trump mean | trick taking games", "In **trick-taking** games, each player plays one card per round, called a **trick**, and the highest card of the suit led usually wins it. A **trump** suit is a special suit that beats all others, so even a low trump can win a trick. Hearts, Spades, Euchre and Bridge are trick-taking games."],
  ["how do you play solitaire | klondike solitaire rules", "In **Klondike** solitaire, deal seven columns with the top card face up. Build columns **downward in alternating colors** (like a red 7 on a black 8). Move aces to four **foundation** piles and build each one up by suit from ace to king. Only a king can go into an empty column. Win by moving all cards to the foundations."],
  ["what is spider solitaire | what is freecell | solitaire variants", "Popular solitaire games:\n- **Klondike**: the classic, alternating colors\n- **Spider**: build complete runs from king to ace in the same suit to clear them, with 1, 2 or 4 suits for difficulty\n- **FreeCell**: all cards are dealt face up, with four free cells for temporary storage; nearly every deal is winnable\n- **Pyramid**: remove pairs that add up to 13"],
  ["solitaire tips | how to win at solitaire", "Solitaire tips: always look for moves that turn over face-down cards, try to empty a column only when you have a king ready, don't rush cards to the foundations if you might need them for building, and play from the larger columns first."],
  ["how do you play blackjack as a game | blackjack rules | what is 21 card game", "In **Blackjack** (also called 21), the goal is to get a hand total closer to **21** than the dealer without going over. Number cards count at face value, face cards are 10 and aces are 1 or 11. **Hit** to take another card or **stand** to stop. Going over 21 is a **bust**. In family games people play just for points or counters."],
  ["how do you play old maid | old maid rules", "In **Old Maid**, remove one queen from the deck, deal all the cards and have everyone discard their pairs. Take turns drawing a card from the player on your left, discarding any new pairs. Eventually one queen is left with no match, and whoever holds it at the end is the **Old Maid**."],
  ["how do you play slapjack | how do you play snap", "In **Slapjack**, players take turns flipping cards onto a pile, and when a **jack** appears, everyone races to slap it; the first to slap wins the pile. In **Snap**, players flip cards onto their own piles, and when two face-up cards match in rank, the first to shout **\"Snap!\"** wins them."],
  ["how do you play spoons | spoons card game rules", "In **Spoons**, put spoons in the middle, one fewer than the number of players. Everyone gets four cards and quickly passes unwanted cards around the circle, trying to get **four of a kind**. When someone does, they grab a spoon, and then everyone else grabs one too. The player left without a spoon gets a letter, and spelling S-P-O-O-N means you're out."],
  ["how do you play speed card game | how do you play egyptian ratscrew", "In **Speed**, two players race to play cards one higher or lower than the cards on two center piles, all at once without turns. In **Egyptian Ratscrew**, players flip cards onto a center pile and slap it on doubles or sandwiches (like 5-K-5); face cards challenge the next player to play a face card in a few tries."],
  ["how do you play cheat card game | how do you play bs card game | i doubt it game", "In **Cheat** (also called **I Doubt It**), players take turns placing cards face down and announcing them in sequence (aces, then 2s, then 3s...). You can lie! Anyone can call **\"Cheat!\"** If the player lied, they pick up the whole pile; if they told the truth, the accuser does. First to empty their hand wins."],
  ["how do you play concentration | memory card game rules", "In **Concentration** (Memory), lay all cards face down in a grid. Take turns flipping two cards. If they match, keep them and go again; if not, flip them back. Remember where cards are! The player with the most pairs at the end wins."],
  ["how do you play euchre | what is euchre", "**Euchre** is a fast partnership trick-taking game using only 24 cards (9 to ace). One suit is named trump, and the jack of trump (**right bower**) and the other jack of the same color (**left bower**) become the highest cards. The team that names trump must win at least three of the five tricks."],
  ["what is bridge card game | how does bridge work", "**Bridge** is a classic partnership trick-taking game for four players. It starts with an **auction**, where players bid how many tricks their partnership will win and which suit will be trump. Then the hands are played. One player's partner, the **dummy**, lays their cards face up on the table."],
  ["how do you play cribbage | what is a cribbage board", "In **Cribbage**, players score points for combinations like cards adding up to 15, pairs, runs and flushes. A wooden **cribbage board** with pegs keeps score. Each round, players also put cards in the **crib**, an extra hand that goes to the dealer. The first to 121 points wins."],
  ["how do you play president card game | how do you play kemps", "In **President**, players race to shed their cards by playing equal or higher cards (or sets) than the last play, and finishing order decides ranks like President and Scum for the next round. In **Kemps**, partners try to get four of a kind and secretly signal each other, and opponents try to catch the signal."],
  ["how many cards are in a deck | what are the suits in a deck of cards | card deck facts", "A standard deck has **52 cards** in four suits: **hearts** and **diamonds** (red), **clubs** and **spades** (black). Each suit has 13 ranks: ace, 2 to 10, jack, queen and king. Many decks also include two **jokers**."],
  ["how do you shuffle cards | how to riffle shuffle | how many shuffles to mix a deck", "Easy shuffles: the **overhand shuffle** (dropping small chunks from one hand to the other) and the **riffle shuffle** (splitting the deck and letting the corners interleave). Math shows about **seven riffle shuffles** mix a deck well. Or just spread cards face down on a table and swirl them around!"],
  ["easy card games for kids | card games for two players | fun card games to play", "Fun card games:\n- **For young kids**: Go Fish, War, Old Maid, Memory, Snap, Slapjack\n- **For two players**: Gin Rummy, Speed, Crazy Eights, Cribbage\n- **For groups**: Spoons, Uno, Cheat, Hearts, Crazy Eights\n- **Solo**: Klondike, Spider, FreeCell"],
  ["sudoku rules | how do you play sudoku", "**Sudoku** is a 9x9 grid divided into nine 3x3 boxes. Fill every empty square with a digit from **1 to 9** so that each **row**, each **column** and each **3x3 box** contains every digit exactly once. No math needed, just logic!"],
  ["sudoku strategy | sudoku tips | how to solve sudoku", "Sudoku strategies:\n- **Scan** rows and columns to see where a number must go in a box\n- Look for **single candidates**: squares where only one number fits\n- Use **pencil marks** to note possible numbers in each square\n- Find **naked pairs**: two squares in a unit that can only be the same two numbers, so remove them from others\n- Never guess; every proper sudoku can be solved by logic"],
  ["what is killer sudoku | what is kenken | what is kakuro", "**Killer sudoku** adds outlined cages whose numbers must add up to a given total. **KenKen** uses cages with math operations (+, −, ×, ÷) and a target number. **Kakuro** is like a crossword with numbers: fill digits 1 to 9 so each run adds up to its clue, without repeating."],
  ["crossword tips | how to get better at crosswords | how to solve crossword puzzles", "Crossword tips:\n- Start with **fill-in-the-blank** clues; they're usually easiest\n- Look at the clue's tense and number: a plural clue usually has a plural answer\n- A question mark means a **pun or wordplay**\n- Fill short words first, then use crossing letters\n- Use pencil (or don't be afraid to erase) and come back later with fresh eyes"],
  ["what is a cryptic crossword | how do cryptic clues work", "A **cryptic crossword** clue has two parts: a normal **definition** (at the start or end) and **wordplay** that also leads to the answer, like anagrams, hidden words or words put together. Indicator words like \"mixed\" or \"broken\" often signal an anagram."],
  ["how do you solve a rubik's cube | rubik's cube beginner method | how to solve a rubiks cube step by step", "The **beginner's layer-by-layer method**:\n1. Make a **white cross** on one face, matching edge colors to the center pieces\n2. Insert the **white corners** to finish the first layer\n3. Solve the **middle layer** edges\n4. Make a **yellow cross** on top\n5. Position and then orient the **yellow corners**\nEach step uses a short memorized move sequence (an algorithm)."],
  ["what is rubik's cube notation | what does r u r' u' mean", "Rubik's cube notation names each face: **R** (right), **L** (left), **U** (up), **D** (down), **F** (front), **B** (back). A letter alone means turn that face **clockwise** a quarter turn (as if you're looking at it), a letter with an apostrophe like **R'** means counterclockwise, and **2** means a half turn. **R U R' U'** is a famous move called the \"sexy move.\""],
  ["rubik's cube facts | who invented the rubik's cube | how many combinations does a rubik's cube have", "The **Rubik's Cube** was invented in **1974** by Hungarian architecture professor **Ernő Rubik**. A standard 3x3 cube has about **43 quintillion** possible positions, yet any of them can be solved in **20 moves or fewer**. Speedcubers use advanced methods like CFOP."],
  ["what is speedcubing | what is cfop method", "**Speedcubing** is solving puzzles like the Rubik's Cube as fast as possible. Many speedcubers use **CFOP**: **C**ross, **F**irst two layers (solved together), **O**rient the last layer and **P**ermute the last layer, using many memorized algorithms."],
  ["jigsaw puzzle tips | how to do a jigsaw puzzle | how to solve a jigsaw puzzle faster", "Jigsaw tips: turn all pieces face up, find and build the **edge pieces** first, sort the rest by color or pattern, work on distinctive areas (like text or bright objects) first, and use the box picture as a guide. Sorting by piece shape helps for big areas of one color, like sky."],
  ["what is a word search | word search tips", "In a **word search**, words are hidden in a grid of letters going across, down, diagonally or even backward. Tip: look for unusual letters like Q, X or Z in your word, or scan row by row for the first letter and check around it."],
  ["what is a nonogram | what is a picross puzzle", "A **nonogram** (also called Picross or paint by numbers) is a grid puzzle. Numbers beside each row and column tell you how many filled squares are in a row there, in order. Use logic to fill in the squares and reveal a hidden picture."],
  ["what is the tower of hanoi | how do you solve the tower of hanoi", "The **Tower of Hanoi** has three pegs and a stack of disks in decreasing size. Move the whole stack to another peg, moving **one disk at a time** and never placing a bigger disk on a smaller one. The minimum number of moves for n disks is **2ⁿ − 1**, so 3 disks take 7 moves."],
  ["what is a tangram | what is a logic grid puzzle", "A **tangram** is a Chinese puzzle of seven flat shapes (five triangles, a square and a parallelogram) that you arrange to form a silhouette, like a cat or a boat, using all pieces without overlapping. A **logic grid puzzle** gives clues, and you mark a grid with yes and no to figure out who owns what."],
  ["what is the 15 puzzle | sliding puzzle tips", "The **15 puzzle** is a 4x4 frame of numbered tiles with one empty space. Slide tiles to put them in order from 1 to 15. Tip: solve the top row first, then the second, then the last two rows together in columns."],
  ["how do you play red light green light | red light green light rules", "One player is the **caller**, standing at the finish line with their back to everyone. When they say **\"Green light!\"** everyone moves forward. When they shout **\"Red light!\"** and turn around, everyone must freeze. Anyone caught moving goes back to the start. The first to tag the caller wins and becomes the next caller."],
  ["how do you play simon says | simon says rules", "The leader gives commands. If they start with **\"Simon says\"** (like \"Simon says touch your nose\"), everyone does it. If the leader gives a command **without** saying \"Simon says,\" anyone who follows it is out. The last player left becomes the new Simon."],
  ["how do you play duck duck goose | duck duck goose rules", "Players sit in a circle while one player walks around tapping heads, saying **\"duck\"** each time. When they say **\"goose!\"**, that player jumps up and chases the tapper around the circle. If the tapper reaches the empty spot without being tagged, the goose becomes the new tapper."],
  ["how do you play musical chairs | musical chairs rules", "Set out chairs in a row or circle, **one fewer** than the number of players. Everyone walks around while music plays. When the music stops, everyone grabs a seat, and whoever's left standing is out. Remove one chair each round. The last player seated wins."],
  ["how do you play hopscotch | hopscotch rules", "Draw a hopscotch court of numbered squares with chalk. Toss a small stone onto square 1, then hop through the court on one foot (two feet where squares are side by side), **skipping the square with the stone**. On the way back, pick up the stone. Then toss it to square 2, and so on. Miss a square or step on a line, and your turn ends."],
  ["how do you play four square | four square rules", "**Four square** is played on a court split into four squares, often ranked King, Queen, Jack and Ace. Players bounce a ball into each other's squares. If the ball bounces twice in your square or you hit it out, you're out, and everyone moves up a square, with a new player entering the lowest one."],
  ["how do you play tetherball | tetherball rules", "In **tetherball**, a ball hangs on a rope from a tall pole. Two players stand on opposite sides and hit the ball in opposite directions. The first player to wrap the rope all the way around the pole wins. No catching or touching the rope!"],
  ["how do you play capture the flag | capture the flag rules", "Split into two teams, each with a territory and a flag. Try to sneak into the other team's side, grab their flag and bring it back to your side without being tagged. If you're tagged in enemy territory, you go to their **jail** until a teammate frees you by tagging you."],
  ["what is freeze tag | how do you play freeze tag | tag variations", "In **freeze tag**, anyone tagged by \"it\" must freeze in place until a free player crawls under their legs or tags them to unfreeze them. Other tag variations include **TV tag** (shout a TV show to be safe), **shadow tag** (step on someone's shadow) and **sharks and minnows**."],
  ["how do you play sharks and minnows | how do you play red rover", "In **sharks and minnows**, one shark stands in the middle while minnows try to run (or swim) across without being tagged; tagged minnows become sharks. In **Red Rover**, two teams hold hands in lines, and the called player runs to try to break through the other line."],
  ["how do you play sardines game | what is sardines hide and seek", "**Sardines** is hide-and-seek in reverse! One person hides, and everyone else searches. When a seeker finds the hider, they quietly squeeze into the hiding spot too. It gets cozier and sillier until the last seeker finds everyone squished together like sardines."],
  ["how do you play marco polo | marco polo game rules", "**Marco Polo** is a pool game. The person who is \"it\" closes their eyes and calls **\"Marco!\"**, and everyone else must answer **\"Polo!\"** The seeker uses the sounds to find and tag someone, who then becomes \"it.\" Always play where an adult is watching."],
  ["how do you play hot potato | hot potato game", "In **hot potato**, players stand in a circle and quickly toss or pass a ball (the \"potato\") while music plays. When the music stops, whoever is holding the potato is out. Keep going until one player is left."],
  ["how do you play telephone | telephone game rules | what is chinese whispers", "In the **telephone** game, players sit in a line or circle. The first person whispers a message to the next, who whispers what they heard to the next person, and so on. The last person says it out loud, and it's usually hilariously different from the original!"],
  ["how do you play 20 questions | twenty questions game", "In **20 Questions**, one player thinks of something. Others ask up to **20 yes-or-no questions** to figure out what it is, like \"Is it alive?\" or \"Is it bigger than a breadbox?\" Guess right before the questions run out to win. Start broad, then narrow down!"],
  ["how do you play i spy | i spy game rules", "One player says **\"I spy with my little eye something that is...\"** and gives a clue, like a color or the letter it starts with. Everyone else looks around and guesses until someone gets it, and then it's their turn. It's perfect for car rides!"],
  ["how do you play hangman | hangman game rules", "One player picks a secret word and draws a blank for each letter. Others guess one letter at a time. Correct letters are filled in; wrong guesses add a part to a stick figure drawing. Guess the word before the drawing is finished to win. Tip: start with common letters like E, A, T and S."],
  ["how do you play pin the tail on the donkey | party games for kids | birthday party game ideas", "Fun birthday party games: **Pin the Tail on the Donkey** (blindfolded, spun around, then try to stick the tail in the right spot), **musical chairs**, **freeze dance**, **hot potato**, **treasure hunts**, **piñata**, **egg and spoon race** and **limbo**."],
  ["how do you play freeze dance | how do you play limbo", "In **freeze dance**, everyone dances while music plays and must freeze like a statue when it stops; anyone who moves is out. In **limbo**, players take turns leaning backward and walking under a pole without touching it, and the pole gets lower each round. How low can you go?"],
  ["how do you do a scavenger hunt | scavenger hunt ideas | treasure hunt clues", "For a **scavenger hunt**, give players a list of items to find, like a pinecone, something red or a round rock, and the first to find everything wins. For a **treasure hunt**, hide clues that each lead to the next spot, ending with a prize. Riddle clues make it extra fun!"],
  ["sack race | three legged race | egg and spoon race | field day games", "Classic field day races: in a **sack race**, hop to the finish inside a sack. In a **three-legged race**, partners tie one leg each together and run in step. In an **egg and spoon race**, carry an egg (or ball) on a spoon without dropping it. **Tug of war** is two teams pulling a rope to drag the other over a line."],
  ["what is tug of war | tug of war tips", "In **tug of war**, two teams pull opposite ends of a rope, trying to drag the center marker over their line. Tips: stay low, lean back, dig your heels in, keep the rope under your arm and pull together in rhythm. Never wrap the rope around your hand."],
  ["how do you play mother may i | how do you play statues", "In **Mother May I**, players ask the leader (\"Mother\") for permission to take moves, like \"Mother, may I take three giant steps?\" Forget to ask and you go back to start. In **statues**, players freeze in funny poses, and the judge tries to make them laugh or move."],
  ["how do you play kick the can | how do you play ghost in the graveyard", "**Kick the can** is a hide-and-seek game where caught players go to jail, but a free player can set everyone loose by kicking the can without getting caught. **Ghost in the graveyard** is played at dusk: one player hides as the ghost, and when found, the ghost chases everyone back to base."],
  ["how do you play jump rope | jump rope rhymes | what is double dutch", "Jump rope can be solo or with two people turning a long rope while others jump in, often chanting rhymes like **\"Cinderella, dressed in yella...\"** **Double Dutch** uses two ropes turning in opposite directions, which takes great timing and is even a competitive sport."],
  ["how do you play marbles | marbles game rules", "In a classic **marbles** game called **ring taw**, draw a circle and put marbles in the middle. Players take turns kneeling outside the circle and flicking a larger **shooter** marble with their thumb to knock marbles out of the ring. Marbles you knock out are yours (or just points, in friendly play)."],
  ["how do you play jacks | jacks game rules", "Scatter the **jacks** on the ground. Toss a small ball up, pick up one jack, and catch the ball after one bounce. Next round, pick up two jacks at a time (\"twosies\"), then three, and so on. Miss the ball or a jack, and it's the next player's turn."],
  ["what is cat's cradle | how do you play cat's cradle", "**Cat's cradle** is a string game for two people. One player makes a pattern with a loop of string on their hands, and the other uses their fingers to take it over, creating a new pattern like the soldier's bed, candles, manger and diamonds."],
  ["what is hacky sack | how do you play hacky sack | what is footbag", "**Hacky sack** (footbag) is about keeping a small beanbag in the air using your feet, knees and chest, without hands. In a circle game, players pass it to each other, trying to keep it up as long as possible. Freestyle players do amazing tricks."],
  ["how do you play four corners | how do you play heads up seven up", "In **four corners**, one person closes their eyes and counts while others choose a corner; then the counter calls a corner, and those players are out. In **Heads Up, Seven Up**, seven players secretly press down the thumb of someone with their head down, and those players try to guess who tagged them."],
  ["how do you play mafia | how do you play werewolf game", "In **Mafia** (or **Werewolf**), players secretly get roles. Each \"night,\" while everyone's eyes are closed, the mafia (or werewolves) secretly pick a player to knock out of the game. Each \"day,\" everyone discusses and votes on who they think is a villain. Villagers win by finding all the villains, and villains win by outnumbering the villagers."],
  ["how do you play two truths and a lie | icebreaker games", "In **Two Truths and a Lie**, each person says three statements about themselves: two true and one false. Everyone else guesses which one is the lie. It's a great **icebreaker**, along with games like **Would You Rather**, **Find Someone Who** and **name games**."],
  ["how do you play the floor is lava | floor is lava game", "When someone shouts **\"The floor is lava!\"**, everyone has to get off the floor fast, onto cushions, chairs or couches, before a countdown ends. Anyone still touching the floor is out. Make sure your perches are safe and stable!"],
  ["how do you play paper rock scissors lizard spock | rock paper scissors lizard spock rules", "**Rock Paper Scissors Lizard Spock** adds two moves: **Scissors** cuts paper, **paper** covers rock, **rock** crushes lizard, **lizard** poisons Spock, **Spock** smashes scissors, **scissors** decapitates lizard, **lizard** eats paper, **paper** disproves Spock, **Spock** vaporizes rock, and **rock** crushes scissors."],
  ["how do you play mash game | what is mash fortune game", "**MASH** (Mansion, Apartment, Shack, House) is a paper fortune game. List options for categories like future home, pet and car. Draw a spiral until someone says stop, count the lines, then count through the list crossing out every item at that number until one is left in each category. That's your silly future!"],
  ["how do you make a cootie catcher | what is a fortune teller paper", "A **cootie catcher** (paper fortune teller) is folded from a square piece of paper: fold corners to the center, flip, fold corners to the center again, then fold in half to open it with your fingers. Write colors, numbers and fortunes inside, and let friends pick to reveal their fortune!"],
  ["what games can i play with no equipment | games to play without anything | car games", "Games with no equipment needed: **I Spy**, **20 Questions**, **Simon Says**, **charades**, **the alphabet game** (find letters A to Z on signs), **would you rather**, **story building** (each person adds a sentence), **rock paper scissors** and **tag**."],
  ["what is the alphabet game | license plate game", "In the **alphabet game**, players look for each letter of the alphabet in order, A to Z, on signs and license plates. In the **license plate game**, you try to spot plates from as many different states or regions as possible."],
  ["indoor games for kids | rainy day games | games to play inside", "Rainy day ideas: **hide and seek**, **sardines**, **freeze dance**, **charades**, **board games**, **card games**, **building a blanket fort**, **balloon keep-up** (don't let it touch the floor!), **indoor scavenger hunt** and **paper airplane contests**."]
);
